The prices of airline tickets and hotels are falling
In the first half of 2008, airline tickets and overnight tickets in Europe were cheaper compared to last year.
According to a current study, airline tickets in Germany went cheap even up to 50 percent.
Airline tickets and overnight stays in hotels went cheap again - this is the result of a study by American Express' travel services and financial services provider.
The company has analyzed its reservation data for the first half of 2008 and after the price increase in the first quarter it found a large change in the second half.
After for many years the prices in the hotel market continued to rise, overnight stays in many places became cheap.
Compared to last year, between January and March prices in 41 of Europe’s 48 different cities rose on average to 6.9 percent.
From April to June prices already fell to 3.6 percent compared last year.
Trendencies were more noticeable in Paris and Rome, where travelers had to pay less than the previous year.
According to American Express, the reason for that was the weakened-dollar course, which was the cause that “the interests of American tourists in traveling declined.”
The decline in hotel prices is also noticed much more in financial centers such as Frankfurt or London than in industrial concentration points such as Hannover, Cologne or Stavanger.
In the Norwegian city hotel prices rose to 26.3 percent and thus break a record in Europe.
In the first quarter, airline ticket prices also showed upward trends:
Due to rising kerosene prices, economy-class travelers had to pay on average 7.1 percent more than in 2007, long-haul flights even became more expensive up to 29.2 percent.
From April to June the prices of airline tickets in the Economy class, compared to the previous quarter, fell on average to 12.5 percent, but price changes for the Businessclass and Firstclass were small.
In Germany the change in prices was more noticeable than in other European countries:
From January to February prices rose to 11.9, in the second half fell to 23.6 percent.
According to a study, prices for domestic flights in Germany even halved.
The cause of the price change is on the one hand an increase in the lazy economy, the company explains.
In many parts of the travel market with the plane, demand is falling.
On the other hand there is great competition for the Open-Skies treaty that liberalizes aircraft traffic between the U.S. and Europe.
The sign of the tense situation is the cancellation of non-profit flights in airlines, as well as a large number of airlines that in the first half of 2008 declared bankrupt - in Europe alone there were 24 airlines.
HSV win against Gladbach 1:0
