Private bank Sal. Oppenheim signed 23 million new shares.
In addition, the commercial and tourism consortium announced that shares in Thoms Cook will not be sold.
Dark matter influences the trajectory of spatial probes
For many years a mysterious phenomenon causes the bewilderment to researchers: the Dark Matter influences the speed of the space probes during the so-called gravitational assistance to the manoeuvre.
Experts fear Albert Einstein’s Theory of Relativity can’t be so correct.
The "Ulysses" space probe spent almost 18 years flying into the universe.
Because the fuel pipes are freezing, it now threatens a death in the cold.
For years a mysterious phenomenon, the anomaly during gravitational assistance to the maneuver, causes bewilderment among researchers.
Gravitational assistance is a manoeuvre of the trajectory of space probes during which they get an acceleration using energy from the gravitational field of a planet, for example from Earth.
This allows space probes to save fuel.
Cassini-Huygens, the European-American space probe used this physique trick just like the Ulysses Sun probe did.
But what surprises researchers for many years: Missiles don’t behave by 100 percent as they are expected according to the venerable law of gravitation.
Rather, they frequent speed irregularities.
Suddenly the probes have greater or lesser speed.
Physicists couldn't explain this effect until now.
They called it the anomaly during gravitational assistance to the manoeuvre.
Experts fear that possibly even Albert Einstein’s General Theory of Relativity which describes the force of attraction of matter by the curvature of space cannot be so correct.
Stephen Adler, the American physicist at Princeton’s Institute for Advanced Study, solemnly believes now that Einstein did not confuse and tries to explain the anomaly during gravitational assistance with the help so-called Dark Mattery, in what astronomers remained in the universe.
“The reason for the anomaly may be the Dark Matter which may also be very close to Earth,” he writes.
Investigators in the universe know the existence of Dark Matter for only ten years.
Supposedly she possesses the force of attraction and her influence can be observed on other planets.
Dark Matter, on the contrary, is completely invisible which means that it does not send and does not reflect any electromagnetic radiation, especially also no visible light.
According to the calculations of scientists the Dark Matter makes at least one-fifth of the total mass of the universe.
Abducted tourists in Egypt are free
As Italy’s foreign minister confirmed, after spending ten days in the hands of hijackers, tourists abducted in Egypt must be released.
Kidnappers in the Sudan had demanded millions of the ransom.
Six kidnappers must be killed in a shooting during the release.
Five Germans are kidnapped in southern Egypt.
Negotiations regarding the rescue have already begun.
The Germans always fall back into the point of attention of the kidnappers.
WELT ONLINE tells about the most important cases.
European tourists abducted in the Sahara are apparently liberated.
About that the same information presents Egyptian state television, the Arab television station Al-Arabija and the Italian media.
After news of the release on Italian broadcaster Sky TG 24 Franco Frattini, the Italian foreign minister confirmed the release: "Our fellow citizens and other hostages are liberated.
"Now we control his state of health," Frattini said.
Five Germans, five Italians and a Romanian as well as their eight Egyptian companions are fine and in safety, state Egyptian television reports.
The 19 participants of the expedition in the desert are on their way to Cairo.
What remains incomprehensible are the reasons for a shooting before the release of the hostages: According to the descriptions of an Egyptian security guard the kidnappers were ready to end the abduction, but the Sudanese army had suddenly killed six kidnappers.
"The problem was solved.
They agreed with the rescue," the guard said.
It was just about "receiving the hostages" whhen suddenly "this surprise" arrived.
On Sunday, Sudanese soldiers killed six kidnappers and two of them have been detained in the border area between Sudan, Chad and Egypt.
The kidnappers have demanded from Germany the ransom in the amount of six million euros; the money had to hand over the German wife of one of the hostages.
The newspaper "El Masri El Jom", referring to a German mediator, reported that the release was delayed because the kidnappers were afraid of their safety.
According to the report, the kidnappers intend to release the femenile hostages after receiving the ransom.
The men had to be detained until the kidnappers had a safe route to flee.
Germany’s embassy in Cairo refused to take the position on this report.
Abuse of stepfather "led to suicide"
According to the court, years of abuse by her stepfather led a woman to kill herself.
Michael Trrise, 57, of Stithians in Cornwall, has been sentenced to five years in prison after he admitted eight counts of indecent assaults.
The Truro Royal Court, through an inquirement study, learned that he had regularly tied his daughter Stephanie Randle, when the girl was between five and seven years old.
Trerise wrote in a confessional paper: "I feel responsible for his death."
The court learned that Ms. Randle committed suicide at her Mutley home in Plymouth on April 1, 2007, at the age of 34.
In the season when the girl lived in Coventry, Trerise used to tie her up and blindfold her before carrying out her sexual acts, while her mother was working in a pub.
After divorce in 1980, Trerise returned to living in Cornwall.
His stepdaughter moved to Plymouth.
However, the court learned that Ms. Randle, who spent much of her time volunteering for the National Trust, was tormented by the abuse that had been inflicted on her twice a week.
In 2006, prosecutor Ramsey Quaife referred to the court what some of Ms. Rande’s friends had said about her, that having been a great surfer and cyclist, she now seemed “emotionally dead,” as if she had “waived life.”
In April 2007 his real father, Terraza Randle, found the dead woman in his Plymouth apartment.
Ms. Rande is supposed to have killed herself by suffocating through a plastic bag.
Mr. Randle confronted his daughter's stepfather, who wrote a confessional letter conceiving, saying, "I used to tie her up and sell her eyes.
It's never been a sexual assault.
“I feel responsible for his death.”
Judge Christopher Elwen replied to Trerise: "You are a very strange fetishist, and because of your victim it must have been a frightening experience, especially when you were only seven years old.
This nasty saga ends with the victim taking off his own life.
Even clearer, if she hadn’t, the issue probably wouldn’t have ended up in court.”
He added: "I've noticed the effect that according to the description made by Stephanie's father and mother the abuse occurred on his daughter over the years.
Clearly she could have had a promising, successful and happy future."
An officer charged by data loss
The BBC was informed that a Cabinet officer who forgot some secret documents on the train in June will be indicted under the Official Secrets Act.
The man, on a working trip through the Ministry of Defense, abandoned two highly classified documents on the train for Waterloo.
The documents were passed to the BBC before being handed over to the police.
Surely it is the least serious charge under the law and does not imply any indication of the intention to delinquire.
BBC defense correspondent Frank Gardner said the conviction had come as a surprise to many in the English administration.
"They thought it was an internal matter between the Ministry of Defense and the Cabinet," he said.
The employee, who cannot be appointed, is loaded under section 8.1 of the Act, the clause relating to the safeguarding of information.
The Royal Legal Service noted that the official must be indicted by the Metropolitan Police, because of the highly sensitive entity of his work on the Secret Services Committee.
Forgotten documents contained secret information about Al-Qaeda and the capacity of security forces in Iraq.
The officer was informed of the decision on Monday morning and immediately moved from his home to a secret location.
Building societies face B&B pricing
In Great Britain construction societies could face a price of more than 80 thousand pounds sterling after the rescue/salvage of Bradford and Bingley bank.
The Government, in effect, provided 14 trillion pounds to protect deposits from the bank’s 2.6 million savers.
Loan interest will be paid by all companies using saver money, including 59 construction companies.
The director general of the Association of Construction Societies (BSA), Adrian Coles, considers "humiliating" that companies and their members have to pay
Eventually, the 14-billion loan should be repaid after Bradford and Bingley’s mortgages are redeemed.
However, the interest that accrues on the interim, is supposed to be charged each year to the 700 financial companies that have deposits.
The Financial Services Compensation Plan confirmed to the BBC that the 59 construction companies are included in that number.
It’s humiliating that the institutions that behaved prudently... are called to pay today the price of institutions that have been much less prudent.
Adrian Coles, BSA
That is to say that each of the companies had to pay in proportion to bank deposits, and that mediation companies had to pay approximately 18% of the cost.
The Treasury estimates that the interest on the loan will amount to 450 thousand sterling pounds in 2009.
This means that societies will have to recover 81 thousand pounds sterling to face the first year of interest.
Adrian Coles, managing director of the Construction Societies Trade Association, told the BBC this situation is unfair.
"It's humiliating that the institutions that behaved prudently in the growth of the real estate market are called to pay the price produced by those that were much less prudent."
According to the BSA, there is no data indicating the failure of any society since 1945.
“We will have the need to examine all aspects of this issue in the coming weeks to find out what options exist to protect building societies and their members from what could be a relevant price,” Cole added.
The interest accrued in 2009 covers seven months from the end of September this year until March.
In 2010, it will be a full year of obligatory interest, which may be almost twice as much.
Being mutual building societies organizations and not having shareholders, their members will inevitably end up paying the price, either through higher rates or lower interests in their savings.
The price of milk rises from five pence per liter
Residents of the Jersey stand will have to pay more for their milk.
The Jersey Dairy has announced that the highest price of dairy products will rise by an extra five pence the litre from next Monday.
It’s this year’s second price increase, after a two-pence increase in February and followed by research carried out by a consulting firm.
Traders can decide for themselves if they want to apply price increases to their customers.
The last time the highest price of milk had risened before February was in 2002.
Former Bangladeshi Prime Minister Denied Bail
A Bangladeshi court has rejected the request for bail for former Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina, in the sense that she can be taken to jail when she returned home.
His defenders said the court did not grant bail accusing extortion of 50 million taka ($729,700) to an entrepreneur when he was Prime Minister.
This is one of several allegations of corruption in his charge.
His long-term political rival, Khaleda Zia, was released on bail earlier this month.
The return of Sheikh Hasina to Bangladesh is supposed to be in the coming weeks, but his party, the Awami League, has demanded that the government of the country, militarily supported, first grant him full freedom.
Correspondents say that while she was given probation to go abroad and receive medical treatments, it’s unclear now what the terms of her release are or whether she will be able to remain free when she returns to Bangladesh.
His lawyers say they will make an appeal to the Supreme Court against the Superior Court’s order.
“The Supreme Court rejected Sheikh Hasina’s request for release on bail, arguing that he couldn’t obtain bail because he was already in treatment abroad on probation,” his lawyer Rafique-ul Huq said.
“We will file an appeal against the Supreme Court’s order.”
Her defenders argued that she needed bail to return home from abroad and prepare for the December elections.
Sheikh Hasina should return to her homeland after Oct. 17, according to an Awami League leader last Sunday.
She was detained last year after the military-backed provisional government took office but was released in June to go abroad and receive medical treatment.
Both Sheikh Hasina and Khaleda Zia reject all charges they are charged.
Fears for the campaign against polio in Afghanistan
Nearly 200,000 children in Afghanistan — the UN says — have failed to take advantage of a recent anti-polio vaccination campaign due to Taliban violence.
Last week the United Nations has carried out a vaccination programme in six provinces of the country.
The organization reports that it was unable to reach the children in some parts of Helmand and Kandahar in the South for safety reasons.
A U.N. spokesman said it occurred despite promises made by the Taliban that would not have attacked or hampered thousands of officials while carrying out vaccinations.
BSkyB Loses Legal Challenge on ITV
BSkyB has lost its legal challenge in the Competition Commission after the decision that forced the company to reduce its participation in the rival ITV broadcast.
BSkyB has been informed by the Court of Competence Appeals that it will have to reduce its 17.9% stake in ITV below 7.5%.
Precedingly, the Commission said BSkyB's interests hinder competition and allowed its unfair influence on ITV.
BSkyB bought its stock package in 2006 in a deal that effectively blocked NTL, now Virgin Media, into buying ITV.
In April 2007, the Anti-Monopoly Office reported that BSkyB’s acquisition of ITV’s stake raised doubts about ITV’s independence and prompted the Competition Commission to open an investigation.
Everyone, including Virgin boss Sir Richard Branson, consumer groups and regulator Ofcom opposed the purchase.
BSkyB, commented that the obligation to reduce its ITV package was unreasonable and constituted a disproportionate remedy, particularly why, BSkyB said, it would have handed over its voting rights.
New investigation in the assumption of U.S. lawyers
The U.S. Attorney-General has appointed a special prosecutor to investigate the firing of nine U.S. attorneys in 2006.
The reason was a report in which the Justice Department’s inspector general criticized the performance by administration officials.
The report criticized White House officials, legislators and former Professor General Alberto Gonzales.
The investigation was motivated by the fear that the lawyers had been removed for political reasons.
Special Prosecutor Nora Dannehy is investigating whether officials questioned during the investigation have lied to investigators in an attempt to obstruct justice.
“Serious allegations of possible criminal conduct have not been fully investigated or resolved,” Attorney General Glenn Bellas states in the report.
One of the main recommendations of the report was the appointment of a special procurator with the power to compel witnesses to leave their statements.
The discussion began when nine U.S. attorneys were removed from office by the Justice Department in December 2006.
At the time, the department prompted the decision by suggesting the attorneys had been fired for poor performance.
But later it became known that the lawyers had not been evaluated worse than other colleagues, and many observers led to the conclusion that layoffs would be motivated for political reasons.
Some of the lawyers in question had worked on the persecution of Republicans, high-profile members of Congress, while others had refused to investigate allegations of electoral fraud against Democrats.
These attorneys investigate and prosecute court cases for the government
and they can be fired at any time, but not removed for political reasons.
The successive head of the Department of Justice, Attorney General Alberto Gonzales, under pressure for the layoffs, renounced his position.
A number of other Justice Department officials did the same.
The Attorney General's report is very critical of Mr. Gonzales ' role in the matter, underlining how he had "primary responsibility" for layoffs, which "abdicated" his leadership position and was "remarkably non-collaborative" in the process.
Other officials, including Karl Rove, Bush’s former aide, were criticized for not cooperating with the investigation.
Meat Loaf's epic of a lot of honor
Rock star Meat Loaf will be honored at the Q Awards for his epic anthem Bat Out Of Hell.
The singer, who will pick up the special award next week at the ceremony to take place in London, said that obtaining this recognition will be "an honor".
The song, which lasts nine minutes and fifty seconds, constituted the title of the album with which Meat Loaf triumphed in 1977.
Coldplay’s group are taking the nominations for this year’s awards and qualifying for all four categories.
Paul Rees, editor-in-chief of Q magazine, said: "There are some songs that transcend time and genre, and Bat Out Of Hell is certainly one of them."
The song, written by Jim Steinman, requires excellent vocal ability such as that used by Meat Loaf.
A nearly two-minute instrumental section opens the anthem.
The song was first known as a single in the UK in 1979 and became No. 15 in qualifying.
However, Meat Loaf’s Bat Out Of Hell remained for about nine years in the rankings and went on to sell more than 40 million copies worldwide.
Meat Loaf - real name Marvin Lee Aday - has suffered health problems in recent years, including a surgical operation for his cardiac arrhythmia in 2003, after a collapse during a concert.
In 2007 an acute laryngitis forced him to cancel some concerts.
It later emerged that he had a cyst on his vocal cord.
Dark city street, often forgotten and perhaps ready for sale
New York City has thousands of avenues, boulevards, streets and other roads, some famous ones, others simply for service.
However, many people, including veteran taxi drivers and longtime local residents, find it difficult to indicate the location of the Extra Place, especially since many of them have never heard of this street.
The small street is located in the East Village and stretches north of the First Street – before I found the Second Street – between the Bowery and the Second Avenue.
This small street, about 30 feet wide and 120 feet long, resembles a dusty alley more than an active road.
There's no signs.
It's practically impossible to find in an atlas.
The street, however, has fans.
For example, there is Avalon Bay, the company that has recently built luxury buildings on Primiera Calle
that wants to repave Extra Place and create a clean hallway to the shops and boutiques that are expected to open in the new buildings.
Other fans include old-data neighbors who said they were thrilled by the truncated lane simply because it is one of the last remnants of the block’s past before the ownership of the buildings.
However, the City’s Department of Housing, Preservation and Development, which is in control of the street, has no interest in owning it.
At this month’s meeting of the Expinity Community Committee that took place this month, representatives of the Avalon agency outlined a proposal to buy the street.
However, the committee, preferring the street to remain in the city, called for a new plan to be unveiled next month, as Susan Stetzer, the district manager of Community n.3.
"The city now owns very few spaces and we would like it to continue to possess Extra Place," said Ms.
"We can consider the proposals made by Avalon to solve the problem and manage the space, but we also want to guarantee public access to the street."
Avalon Bay had announced several months ago that he wanted to repair the small street and install some seats, including some to be used as coffee, waiting to rent the spaces.
The idea that Avalon could buy the street first came about during this month’s meeting
but Michele de Milly, a spokeswoman for Avalon Bay, said that even though the company wanted to use the street, he didn’t necessarily want to own it.
"Avalon Bay's primary concern is the renovation of this abandoned alley and the possibility of turning it into a public area," he said, adding that the sale proposal came from the city itself.
Seth Donlin, a spokesman for the Committee for Preservation, confirmed that it was the city’s desire to sell the street and noted that the agency’s mission is the development of the property rather than its management.
Compared to its surroundings more color, those of the Bowery, Extra Place has left an imprint on history.
Some believe that it got its name in 1802, when Philip Minthorne’s large estate was divided among his sons and this narrow strip of land was not claimed by anyone.
The back door of the CBGB, the punk rock memorial that closed in 2006, opened in Extra Place, but the street is perhaps best known for the photograph of the establishment coming out on the deck of Ramones "Rocket to Russia".
"The environment was magnificent," said Danny Fields, the Ramones' manager, who took the photograph in November 1976.
"It was full of garbage, pieces of clothes and pieces of barrels, posters, leaves, ropes."
Some residents, including bloggers who write about the gentrification of the East Village, have objected to the idea of selling Extra Place, arguing that public goods, still modest, must remain public.
Others have praised Extra Place as a rare piece, virtually unchanged, a cratch of the city that should be tutored.
Jeremías Moss, who runs a blog called Jeremiah's Vanishing New York ("The Book of Lamentations: A Bitterly Nostalgic Look at a City in the Process of Going Extinct"), has made available part of a talk of the city's history about Extra Place, published by The New Yorker in 1952.
The author, Brendan Gill, wrote: "Extra Place is a slightly narrow and dead end, also dark for the day and marked by rusty and counter-window iron warehouse doors, with old newspapers of weeks blown along the gutters."
With few exceptions, the street seemed roughly the same on a recent afternoon.
A rat heals through the back of the street and stood nearby the door that was used to enter the CBGB and that now houses a luxury clothing store.
Graffiti on the walls and an empty bottle of beer about 22-oz were close to a new batch of discarded newspapers.
Some interns said they rarely paid attention at Extra Place, but suggested it could be cleaned.
Mr. Fields, meanwhile, noticed that some people underestimate the attractive power of the street in its current state.
"I spent the best years of my life," he said, "in dark, damp and disgusting places."
Debate on bilingualism: immersion in the English language
In this chapter of Education Watch, Bruce Fuller and Lance T. Izumi discuss candidates’ positions on bilingual education.
Lance T. Izumi, an old associate in California studies and director of studies at the Pacific Research Institute for Public Policy, is co-author of the book "Not as Good as You Think: Why the Middle Class Needs School Choice."
Effectively calling Hispanic voters is a difficult business.
The education proposals made by Barack Obama are an example of that.
Mr. Obama’s campaign notes that “African-Americans and Latino students are significantly less likely to graduate than white students,” and this is a very true consideration.
To combat such differences in learning events, Obama’s education plan specifically advocates, among other things, “bilingual transitional education” for English-pupils.
However, the open question for Mr. Obama is whether the commitment of bilingual education, which prefers instruction in languages other than English in classroom, is effective in nullifying the distance in the final results.
Take, for example, Sixth Street Prep, an officially recognized elementary school in eastern Los Angeles County.
In this school the vast majority of students are Hispanic and low-income families:
More than a third of students, many of whom are newcomers, are learning the English language.
However, an incredible 100 percent of fourth-year students have been positively evaluated on the 2008 math exam
and almost an equally staggering 93 percent have mastered the English language and arts test.
This incredible success has been achieved using a different ingredient than that favored by Mr. Obama.
Sixth Street School emphasizes review and practice, constant evaluation of competencies and does not tolerate any attitude of excuses.
In addition, and this is where Mr. Obama should take note, according to Linda Mikels, the principal of the Sixth Street, the school’s approach to English instruction to students is in “total immersion.”
Immersion in English means the almost exclusive use of the language in the treatment of the contents of the instruction.
Ms Mikels, who opposes bilingual education, told me, "we've had extraordinary successes with students like the last one who's arrived from Mexico and who, if you walked into the classroom 12 months later you wouldn't be able to pick up what it was."
"It works," he said, "we think it works."
Will Mr. Obama take a school like Sixth Street Prep as a model to replicate for other schools with large populations of Hispanic-English students?
The results achieved by this school should remove any doubts about the answer, but the idea of his party’s politicians on the issue of education (the Associations for National Education have long been advocates of bilingual education) and their very constant support for dark bilingual education the prediction of Mr Obama’s response.
While immigrants must learn English, Mr. Obama has recently trivialized the issue by letting people stop worrying about legislation that is only written in English
Instead, he said, "you need to make sure that your child can speak Spanish."
If Mr. Obama really wants to improve the system and close loopholes, he should carefully consider well-working education models rather than despising or trivializing them.
The price of oil falls sharply after renewed economic fears
The price of oil fell sharply on Monday because of concerns that the U.S. bailout plan for financial markets may not be able to reactivate the economy.
Crude oil shares declined from 7 percent to US$99.80 a barrel in morning trading on the New York Mercantile Exchange.
The price has fallen by more than 20 dollars per barrel since last Monday.
Over the past two weeks, commodity markets have been shaken by the financial crisis on Wall Street and by a pair of powerful hurricanes in the Gulf of Mexico.
After peaking at US$145.29 per barrel in July, prices had fallen to nearly US$90 a barrel earlier this month, before retreating, once again, due to enormous uncertainty in financial markets.
Anxiety seized investors once again on Monday, despite congressional leaders they said had reached an agreement over the weekend on the bailout plan, the largest in history.
The news was overshadowed by new concerns that the financial crisis would be far from over.
The evolution that occurred in the last episode of the merger process confirms that Citigroup will buy the banking operations of the Wachovia Corporation, according to what the government said on Monday.
In the meantime, the Belgian, Dutch and Luxembourg governments have partially nationalized the European financial conglomerate Fortis.
Analysts at Barclays Capital said that, on the frantic weekend, negotiations leading up to the bailout agreement "seem not to have been able to reactivate market sentiment."
As the economic situation deteriorates, it is expected in a recess in demand for commodities, including oil.
“The outlook for global equity, the interest and exchange rate is going to become increasingly uncertain,” Deutsche Bank analysts wrote in a note to investors.
"We believe that commodities will not be able to escape this contagion.
From a product perspective, our most pressing concern is the assessment whether the American virus will spread globally and specifically to China.”
The bank’s analysts have lowered their expectations for next year, such as low oil consumption because of the crisis in economic growth and the forecast to reduce the price of oil and gas is 20 percent by 2009.
Second-quarter of madness: team sets record
Having passed, over the past eight seasons, longer than anyone in Jets' uniform, playing in defense, Shaun Ellis thought I'd seen everything.
Then he watched things unfold in the second quarter this Sunday, when the Jets transformed an unscored game into a deflating game.
"I've never seen anything like that," Ellis said.
The second quarter began after the Jets and the Arizona Cardinals had played lazily under the gloomy of the clouds.
At this moment it happened that quarterback Brett Favre found Laveranues Coles making a 12-yard goal and suggesting the perfect omen for an afternoon, in which the evolution of relationship between the players had a great leap forward.
Favre found Coles for two more goals in the fourth, one of these when one cornerback fell and another arrived with 10 seconds in the middle.
"I didn't get out of my way," Favre said of all the footsteps that went to Coles.
"He's a devil of a player.
Our communication has been great regardless of what people can think."
While Favre and Coles were in the dam, the Cardinals' defense mistreated quarterback Kurt Warner.
Cornerback Darrelle Revis intercepted a sloping shot and returned him 32 yards for a touchdown.
Safety Eric Smith pointed to another pass and intercepted him.
Linebacker David Bowens was forced into a foul by Warner.
In the last turnover, the fouls forced by Bowens, could have been the best.
The Cardinals had taken possession with 10 seconds available, and after the Jets regained the ball, Jay Feely kicked him 20-yard to win a definitive 34-0.
They're the biggest points the Jets never made in a room.
Team coach Eric Mangini asks him to describe the madness of this moment, said: "The second quarter or the third quarter?"
When both were answered - the Jets allowed 21 unanswered points in the third quarter - Mangini said: "I can say that I liked the second quarter much more than the terzero.
They’ve earned a lot of points in a very short period of time.”
Gebrselassie rested leads his world record
Once again, Ethiopia’s Haile Gebrselassie has demonstrated that many consider him the greatest runner in history, surpassing his own world record in the Berlin marathon on Sunday and becoming the first person capable of running the 26.2-mile race in 2 hours 4 minutes.
After the victory in the accommodatious flat race in 2:03:59, Gebrselassie has shattered by 27 seconds his previous record of 2:04:26, obtained last year in Berlin.
At the age of 35, Gebrselassie has set the 26th world record in a race of an impressive length so he has set a world championship or a world record for any distance, from the time trial to the marathon.
Last month, Gebrselassie hasn’t taken part of the Olympic marathon in Beijing, fearing – perhaps too alarmingly, in retrospect – that air pollution would aggravate its problems with asthma.
Instead, he signed up to race in Berlin.
In practically perfect conditions - sunny and with 48 degrees at the beginning of the race, 55 degrees at the end - Gebrselassie won this Sunday, employing on average a sharp 4 minutes 43 seconds per mile.
Since when runners like Gebrselassie and Paul Tergat of Kenya have converted over long distances, the marathon record has shrunk by more than two minutes in the last decade, as Ronaldo da Costa of Brazil won in Berlin at 2:06:05 in 1998.
After Sunday’s victory, Gebrselassie told reporters that he had suffered calves in the calf two weeks earlier and that he had lost a week of training.
It is evident that the suspension left him fresh, unoxidized.
"I'm very happy," Gebrselassie told reporters.
"Everything was perfect.
I started again a week ago, and today I dyed a few doubts, but in the end everything went well."
The athlete has been the subject of some criticism - especially in Kenya, Ethiopia's Afro-Eastern rival - for the style of orchestrating their careers too much, in general, avoiding the encounter with many world marathon stars, based on the pacemakers as if it were a race track, and setting the records ahead of the sense of competition.
But this critique doesn’t diminish the grandeur of Gebrselassie’s skills as runner.
This Sunday, at the march of four Kenyans, Gebrselassie reached the middle of the race in 1:02:04.
Twen Kenyan elite runners, James Kwambai and Charles Kamathi, began challenging Gebrselassie after their pacemakers declined.
But shortly before three miles of the goal, Gebrselassie picked up a final push to split from Kwambai, who finished second at 2:05:36, improving his best personal record for nearly five minutes.
Kamathi peaked at third in 2:07:48.
In the women’s race, Irina Mikitenko, native of Kazakhstan but now a German citizen, completed a remarkable year winning in 2:19:19, the seventh fastest time in history.
Paula Radcliffe of Great Britain holds the world record of 2:15:25.
Last spring, Mikitenko, 36, was a surprise and won the London Marathon in 2:24:14.
She also didn’t take part in the Beijing Games so she was described as a back injury, but she recovered to win in Berlin and set her best-record staff in nearly five minutes in the third marathon she raced.
He did an intelligent calculus race, leaving a quartet of Kenyan and Ethiopian runners to make their heads and then recovering them after 18 1⁄2 miles.
Ethiopia’s Askale Tafa Magarsa won second place in 2:21.31, while Kenya’s Elena Kiprop finished third in 2:25:01.
In a separate city, the pride and trials of colored life
Hidded by the expansion of the Orlando-themed park, a few miles from the bright, noisy and colossal city, there’s a quiet town, where the pride and complications of the African-American experience become very apparent.
Eatonville, Zora Neale Hurston's first fully black-inhabited city and children's place, is no longer as simple as she described it in 1935: "the city of five lakes, three croquet fields, 300 men of color, 300 good swimmers, many guavas, two schools and not a prison."
Now it's a place of pilgrimage.
Alice Walker, Toni Morrison and Ruby Dee have come to the annual Zora! Fair in Eatonville to pay tribute to Ms. Hurston, the most famous Harlem Renaissance writer.
And yet, in many ways, the city she described – also making it a tourist stage through its inclusion in Florida’s travel guide produced by the Depression Season Federal Writers Project – is still a separate place.
The city stays independent, dignified and private as it was in the 30’s, when Mrs. Hurston wrote that rural blacks in Florida often resistant to sharing their true ideas with the white man, who “knows so little about us and who doesn’t know what he’s losing.”
Even now, in the year in which a black presidential candidate, Senator Barack Obama, has drawn attention to beginning a dialogue on the issue of race, many are still wary in front of the outsider.
“We’re very cautious about the way our story is told,” said Jones Hortense, 59, a lifelong resident and member of the city’s oldest church.
"It has to be right."
Eatonville has always been defined a paradox of triumph and struggle.
At the same time it is a historical model of the affirmation of blacks and a community of almost 2,400 people who live under a level of poverty twice the national average.
It’s a literary center, but also a repaired example of black southern rural culture – sometimes disdained, others praised – that was born out of slavery in America.
It’s no wonder, if those residing here are both proud and reserved.
And the concern for Eatonville's image really began with Zora, as no one calls her Mrs Hurston here.
She introduced her hometown to the world through her deep books in dialect as "Mules and Men" (1935) and "Their Eyes Were Watching God" (1937).
Five paragraphs in Florida’s guidance transformed the city, just off Route 17, a highway that runs through central Florida often forgotten into the history of blacks and human drama.
Faced with cement both in life and literature, Ms. Hurston took her readers to Eatonville's first mayoral's owned store, Joe Clarke, and then into more private areas.
"Off-the-road to the left," he wrote, "there's the modern brown public school with its white finishes, its well-maintained gardens and playgrounds, cared for by Howard Miller, as he can barely read and write."
She also mentions the widow Dash's new husband and writes that Glenn Lee "sells drinks of all kinds and anything goes with transient rooms."
Thus, in just a few hundred words, Ms Hurston relates Eatonville to self-government, but also to illiteracy, second marriage and sex.
Evidently, this Fodor didn't.
In fact, not everyone appreciated the portrait.
"Zora described it as it was," said Ella Dinkins, 90, one of the Johnson girls that Mrs. Hurston immortalized citing men who were singing colorless songs about their beauty.
He added: "Some people didn't like that."
Ms. Hurston is still remembered here as a lively eccentricle who returned frequently after her family moved to Jacksonville, Florida.
Franklin Augusto, 77, is remembered that when Ms. Hurston arrived in town, she did so without warning in a big Chevrolet, smoking and wearing pants in a city she is still proud to dress with dignity.
Most residents were fascinated, Mr. Franklin said, while many laughed sarcastically.
"People were always very happy to see Zora," follows Mr. Franklin.
But, he adds, swaying in her chair in a backyard overlooking Lake Sabelia, where Ms. Hurston was probably baptized, “she never stayed too long.”
When Ms. Hurston died in 1960, she was poor and her books were no longer stamped.
Like most of the world, Eatonville also seemed to have forgotten about it.
Although she was a literary star, a contemporary of Langston Hughes and the only woman of color at Barnard College in the 1920s, she was buried in an anonymous tomb in Fort Pierce-Florida, where she had been living.
In Eatonville, there were no great memorial ceremonies, no major public readings.
“I don’t think his contribution to the world or his heritage was understood,” said Valerie Boyd, author of “Wrapped in Rainbows,” a biography of Ms. Hurston published in 2003.
A certain stealth came in the 80's.
Orange County officials wanted to put a five-lane highway through the city to replace Kennedy Boulevard, the labor community's main two-lane street.
The expansion of the Orlando area had already pushed Interstate 4 across the western edge of the city.
The proposal came at a time when Eatonville was recovering from a difficult period in its history.
Forced integration, among other things, had terminated relatively idyllic isolation from the community.
In the 50’s, the struggle for racial mix brought hatred closer to the community.
“During that season, a group of white boys were going through the city throwing oranges and everything against the people who were sitting next door,” Franklin said.
“They killed a girl too, once.
He was thrown against a watermelon in the car.”
In a 1955 letter to Mrs. Hurston he wrote to The Orlando Sentinel, criticizing the forced integration by the Supreme Court that Brown v. Board of Education “insulted instead of honoring my race.”
R Residents say today that the desegregation of schools, positive in some respects, had weakened Eatonville’s cohesion and undermined the confidence of its youth.
“Black kids were used to being hugged — I remember this — it used to hug the teacher in the morning and evening,” said N.Y. Nathiri, Ella Dinkins’ daughter and CEO Preserve the Eatonville Community, a non-profit organization.
This lasted, he added, until teachers and students came from the same place.
"I wasn't hugging your teacher because it was white - there's a cultural gap there," Ms. Nathiri said.
Civil rights, however, helped create space for many more Zora Neale Hurstons – black writers, actors and artists who passed over prejudice, just as she did, with buoyant, confidence, and lines like: “How can anyone deny himself the pleasure of my company!
I don’t understand.”
In 1975, writer Alice Walker went to Mrs. Hurston’s anonymous grave and began struggling to resurrect her reputation.
Five years later, an acclaimed biography of Ms. Hurston by Robert E. Hemenway appeared in bookstores, reintroducing her into American canons.
The road project came just the moment Eatonville’s most famous daughter was back in the spotlight.
And this time, Ms. Hurston’s old neighbors saw her as a savior.
In 1988, the community began planning a fair for Ms. Hurston to show what the county could ruine with that highway.
Thousands of admirers arrived at the inaugural event two years later, and every January, many return for celebration.
After several years, the county has abandoned its highway proposal.
"The resurrected idea of the five-way road, it would have been to put into what you would call curvature speed, a true civic pride," Ms. Nathiri said.
Ms. Boyd said it more simply: "Zora saved Eatonville."
The victory over the road project has helped change the image of the city itself.
People from abroad like Rachelle Munson, an attorney who began coming to church here in 1993, began appearing in large numbers, and residents began reviewing the past.
Eatonville entered the National Historical Register in 1998.
A new library (carrying Hurston's name, of course) opened in 2006, about a repaved and embellished Kennedy Boulevard.
Today, Eatonville remains an anomaly in Florida: Just six miles from downtown Orlando, you can sometimes feel like on a street of the past in a summer rain, in a city as small as the one that was founded by only 27 black families in the 1880’s.
(90 percent of people are black today.)
Strangers who come in search of Eatonville's history, of its meaning, are often still treated with caution.
An advance permit is necessary to do most of the interviews, and some things - such as the murals at the oldest church in Eatonville, painted by a white man and showing black men working in the field - cannot be photographed.
Many in Eatonville, such as Ms. Jones, a courageous and convinced teacher who has a weakness for bright red, still fears that her island community gives can be misunderstood.
And yet, as the Hurston festival has expanded, a higher level of native pride has also arisen.
Young people, in particular, tend to see Eatonville in the same way that Mrs Hurston saw her entire race: beauty, and all the problems, not better, not worse, and equally to the other racial and ethnic groups in America, proud, creative, hardworking, silly and mixed.
It’s a sincere civic sense what can be heard in the voice of Mr. Franklin’s nephew Edwin Harvey, 18, who is planning to return to Eatonville after college to work in the local government or by the Police Department, which he said might need some help.
And even those who are younger, like Alondra and Alexia Kenon, two Winter Park 11-year-old twins, seem to have learned to describe Eatonville correctly.
“Most people, in case they dare go here, they’ll have to think, ‘Oh, this city is nothing compared to anyone else,’” Alondra Kenon says after going to church on a recent Sunday.
"But if you really stand up and take a moment to look at its history, it's a very nice city."
Over here in New York, everyone is currently talking about finances
On the surface, nothing has changed.
If tourists in Manhattan stayed away from kiosks and gigantic TV screens in Times Square, they wouldn’t realize finding themselves in the eye of the hurricane.
They could never imagine that behind the glass façade of bee-nested offices above their heads, bankers and speculative fund managers monopolizing New York clash daily with the worst “office day” since 1929’s slump.
Boys for home delivery keep resonating their metal tea carts through the sidewalks and carrying muffin trays for breakfast meetings.
Latino men continue dragging between cargo apartment buildings, as well as human mules, with 50 eccentric print shirts hanging a yoke through their shoulders. Office workers still wait in queue for their morning take-away, and it is still impossible to take a taxi between 4 and 7 in the afternoon on business days.
Employees are still waiting to queue for their bagel to the sellers’ carts, and it’s still impossible to find a taxi between four and five o’clock in the afternoon in the days of the week.
It seemed to me as if there was no crisis at all, until, this Thursday afternoon, with all the pessimistic forecasts that there are, I have decided to open a new savings account.
Although I had calculated the time it takes for the operation to coincide with the calm at the end of the afternoon in my local JP Morgan Chase subsidiary, a queue of about 20 people was already waiting.
And not ahead of the employee’s window, to withdraw their cash or make a deposit, but in front of the customer services bank, where lifelong customers order their checkbooks, tourists are looking for instructions to go to Macy’s department stores and new customers want to open their accounts.
Most of these people preceded me, I was later informed by Starr, my "trustworthy personal banker", and they were in queue for the same reason as me.
Unlike me, they had already withdrawn their savings from the smallest banks in places from where they had commuted them, places like New Jersey, Pennsylvania and Staten Island.
They had taken their money out of the so-called savings associations (a bit like a construction society), from the credit unions and savings banks of their hometown whose stripes only work within the state of belonging, and who claim to treat customers like their family.
Over the past year, 15 of these banks have quietly failed.
In recent days, Washington Mutual, a bank with $327 billion of assets, went bankrupt, and as New Yorkers headed home Friday night for the weekend, another bank, Wachovia, with $440 billion savings deposits, was looking for a ransom buyer.
Workers in the offices and tents in front of me, fearing another collapse, were shifting their money to a bank to what’s considered a safe haven, which is doing most of the ransoms on Wall Street.
Ordinary people from New York have in recent weeks become financial experts.
A woman at my local Chinese supermarket, speaking on the phone, was referring to Lehman Brothers, the failed bank, and its grieved-looking chief executive, Dick Fuld, commented on the matter familiarily as complaining about a colleague’s inactivity.
And Lyn, fiftieth and somewhat janitored my apartment block, has changed the normal course of our conversations about Brad and Angelina or my shopping to the shopkeeper.
He told me, after my visit to the bank, that "I now have all my money market fund savings.
Commutation's done, I'm not taking any risks."
Desecrated Muslim tombs as Austria approaches right
Heinz-Christian Strache, from the left wing of the far-right party for the freedom of Austria and Belgian Filip Dewinter, affiliated with the far-right Vlaams Belang party, take part in a demonstration to present the organization "Cities against Islamization" in Antwerp, Thursday, January 17, 2008.
They carry traffic signs depicting mosques with a red line going through.
Police are blaming far-right extremists for desecrating a Muslim cemetery in Austria the same weekend that far-right political parties made huge gains in the country’s general elections.
More than 90 graves were severely damaged at Traun Cemetery near Linz sometime between Friday night and this morning in what police believe was an organized action.
Criminals sprinkled Jewish symbols, such as the Star of David on some of the graves, but investigators think this may have happened by an attempt to hide the real motives of hate-driven extremists against Muslim immigrants.
It’s not clear whether the incident was somehow related to elections.
A spokesman for the local Islamic Community said they were "deeply shocked" by the news of the desecration, which reached the Muslim religious when Ramadan month was running out.
In the meantime, Austria is preparing for a round of introspection then the right-hand turn in yesterday’s parliamentary elections.
The two far-right parties that claimed nearly 30 percent of the vote, the Freedom Party and the Alliance for the Future of Austria, had conducted a campaign focused on anti-immigration vehemence and some of their slogans were considered xenophobic by critics.
Heinz-Christian Strache, the leader of the Freedom Party who won more than 18 percent of the vote, campaigned against headed handkerchiefs and burqas, and also voiced his opposition to food considered Islam-related.
At his last meeting in Vienna, he spoke of a "European brotherhood" to prevent the rise of Islam.
Both parties try to ban the construction of mosques and minarets, arguing that they were symbols of the ongoing Islamization policy in Austria and Europe.
Polls and analyses carried out immediately after the elections, which have confirmed the far-right as the country’s strongest political bloc, indicate that the change occurred mainly by young voters who are worried about their future in the European Union.
Prohibition of travel abroad for Gary Glitter
Courts have banned Gary Glitter, the fascinating rock star guilty of child sexual abuse, of traveling to France and Spain.
Magistrates imposed a six-month ban on Glitter after he informed surveillance authorities that he wanted to travel to Spain through France.
Kent police filed a travel ban abroad last Thursday after the magistrates had produced evidence of Glitter’s previous convictions for sexual abuse of children.
Glitter, real name, Paul Gadd, did not attend the hearing at the Ashford Magistrates Court in Kent, but was represented by lawyer Corker Binning.
“On Thursday, Sept. 25, at the Ashford Magistrate Court in Kent, the police requested a disposition to prevent Mr. Paul Gadd’s travels abroad as to France and Spain until March 25.
"Paul Gadd didn't attend in person."
Glitter, 64, had informed authorities that he wished to travel abroad under the register of sex offenders.
He must inform the authorities if he wishes to travel abroad for more than three days
and it is also necessary to communicate to the police the scheduled date seven days in advance of their departure.
Kent police said they would not discuss Glitter's individual case or possible whereabouts in the county.
A Kent police spokesman said: "Kent police will do their best to protect children in this country, or abroad.
A Kent police spokesman said: "Kent police will do their best to protect children in this country, or abroad.
“From our point of view, it’s easier to keep an eye on someone who stays in the UK because here we have the systems to manage all possible risks more effectively.
"Protecting the inhabitants of Kent is the main concern of the Kent police and we work closely together to monitor the behavior of sex offenders registered in the county and minimize any risks that may exist for the public."
Glitter returned to the UK last month after spending 27 months in a Vietnamese prison for the abuse of two girls, aged between 10 and 11.
Glitter was released he flew to Bangkok, where he took a flight to London.
However, he said that he felt bad and refused to fly to London.
The Thai authorities did not allow him to pass through passport control and was eventually blown up to Hong Kong.
Chinese authorities also denied him entry and he flew back to Bangkok, where Thai police and immigration officials insisted that he returned to Britain.
He probably arrived at Heathrow Terminal 3 on 22 August and remained there while his lawyer David Corker went to Uxbridge Magistrate Court, west of London, after Scotland Yard had successfully applied the order to register as a sex offender.
Glitter had been jailed in the UK for four months in 1999 for possession of images of child abuse.
A source from the Ministry of Internal Affairs reported that Jacqui Smith, the Minister of the Interior calmed down and rejoiced in knowing that Glitter had been banned from traveling to France and Spain.
"I capture the off-scene master to extract his character" Günther Bauer_Photographer
No heels nor ruffles or premiere suits.
The essence of the great masters of flamenco, stripped of concentration in the interpretation to obtain the relaxed appearance of the man or woman.
On one side the artist.
To the other, the person.
Inseparable except in privacy, in those moments oblivious to the public, when to find the privacy of a gesture, the depth of a stolen look or the expressiveness of some hands, unconcerned because they are not going to go on the scene.
German Günther Bauer alternates in "Flamenco-people" the theatricality of some stagings with singers, dancers and guitarists with that lesser-known side of these masters.
With this proposal, the photographer collects the particular iconography of flamenco from different fields.
Included in the programming of Flamenco Photography Month, the exhibition "reflects the international importance of flamenco, greater every day", explains the creator, who clarifies that "Flamenco-people" "can be any people but, upon seeing the photos, the observer realizes that they are not the typical images of artists on stage.
These people portrayed "in the street" are artists capable of transmitting on stage passion, sadness, joy ... captivating the audience.
To capture the personality of these artists you don’t have to take pictures of the stage.
They carry their personality and character inside.
What interested me was to create an intimate atmosphere that would allow me to capture with the camera the personality of the greats of flamenco."
With the flavor of the "Cante de las Minas" festival, Bauer began in 1,997 a long journey to portray singers, guitarists or dancers with a plaque chamber.
They are portraits that, due to the selection of the camera and the material remember the classical photography.
The application of different stylistic media breaks with the classical basis of the image, conferring modernity, an idea that reflects how Bauer himself understands flamenco: "A music with very traditional roots, which evolves and surprises every day".
It is not the action of dancing, singing or touch itself, but the personality of the artist, his character, that captivates with his art to the public.
The search for an encounter with the essentials, with the goblin that is made visible in a way of feeling, being and being are and have been the constant of this work that is presented these days in the gallery El Fotomata.
Well-known flamenco figures such as Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales, Vicente Amigo, among others, configure this selection of almost seventy artists photographed.
The project has become an anthology of flamenco, which will soon be published in a book that will collect the portraits exhibited in the exhibition.
Bauer's passion for portraits led him to repeat the idea two years ago with "SportARTproject".
Although the concepts are different in "Flamenco-people", the photographer admits that "I love to portray people, meet them and be able to enter a moment in their life, whether it is a long or short moment.
I think that within photography portraiture is the most difficult genre.
You meet or confront people, characters, that sometimes you don’t even know, or that leave you just a few minutes to take the photo.
Despite these circumstances, as a photographer, I always try to make my ideas come true."
This collection of portraits of the current Flamenco entitled "Flamenco-people" is exhibited in the El Fotómata Gallery, located at number 20 of Calle de la Mata, parallel to the Alameda de Hércules, where it can be visited until October 16.
Vargas Llosa confesses that the novel he writes is "a recklessness"
It is set in places that he does not yet know and deals with unknown issues, but still, Mario Vargas Llosa is bent on carrying out the new project in which he works, a novel starring Roger Casemant, an Irish nationalist who lived twenty years in the Congo and passed through the Amazon.
The always favorite of the Nobel Prize in Literature confessed yesterday in his passage through the Hay Festival Segovia that the project that today concerns him "is a recklessness" and that "everything indicates that he should not write" the novel.
"If it was sensible, I wouldn't do it, but I'm going to do it and I hope to finish it some time," he concluded in his speech at the four-day exchange of ideas, thoughts and words festival that for four days has seen a hundred writers, historians and journalists and thousands of people pass through the city of the Aqueduct.
The author of "The City and the Dogs" was one of the outstanding figures of the third edition of the Feast of the Word and fulfilled the forecasts.
Vargas Llosa, who has won the Hay Festival award for being the most voted writer by the participants - an old edition of "Our mutual friend", by Dickens-, filled the capacity of the Juan Bravo theater.
Presented by Félix Romeo, he found before him an audience dedicated to the one that became very short the hour in which the Peruvian, with a prodigious memory and with great doses of humor, reviewed how his literary vocation was born in it, derived from the "extrordinary adventure" of the "pleasure of reading" and "living more than he lived"; how he writes in a "disciplicated way"
The Spanish-American novelist acknowledged that "there's no way" to measure and know if books change life, "but I deny myself that literature is pure entertainment, an ephemeral experience," said Vargas Llosa, who said it "has effects" and "makes people more unhappy", so he asked the writers "not to abandon themselves to frivolity" and "assume with responsibility" their vocation.
The climatology undermines the Pozoblanco fair that also runs out
The festivity of rejones that closed the subscription of the Pozoblanco fair was suspended yesterday morning, after checking the rejoneadores announced that the floor of the square was not in the best conditions.
In this way, the company, according to Fermín Bohórquez, Pablo Hermoso de Mendoza and Leonardo Hernández, decided to suspend the celebration of the race.
Inclement weather has been primed with this fair, since finally two of its three celebrations have not been celebrated.
On Saturday it was the rain that prevented the celebration of the second scheduled bullfight.
The poster was composed by José Luis Moreno, Curro Díaz and Juan Bautista, who were going to face a confinement of Castilblanco
So, only on Friday there was a celebration.
In it, the winner was Daniel Luque who cut three ears and went out on his shoulders, after leaving passages with taste and aesthetics.
That very afternoon Alejandro Talavante settled his twoughs with greetings and an ear, who conceded more the lines than his bullfight.
Of course, even worse was El Juli, who went through the thing of Los Llanos without sorrow or glory.
In his second bull, after a task of relief won the anger of a large part of the public.
They skip a control in Álava in a van with two thousand kilos of hashish
Five minus ten in the morning.
Traffic control of the Ertzaintza in the municipality of Armiñón (Álava).
The agents stop the two occupants of a van and they flee.
Its cargo, more than two tons of hashish, wasn’t enough to stop.
Officers pursued them and arrested one of the two occupants, a 28-year-old man, identified as A.A.J., according to the Basque Government’s Interior Department.
The arrested individual has been charged with drug trafficking and falsifying a public document.
The incident was recorded when several officials of the Álava Traffic Unit were carrying out a transport control on the N-1, as they passed through the municipality of Armiñón.
They were preparing to stop a van for inspection when the driver disobeyed the orders, accelerated sharply, and skipped control.
Officers began their pursuit until the vehicle stopped a couple of kilometers later on the shoulder, at which time its two occupants got away to flee to the race in a wooded area.
The ertzainas finally gave reach to one of them and seized the 2,178 kilograms of hashish they were transporting and found after inspecting the van, an Iveco Daily.
The back of the vehicle was loaded with bundles of hashish, so they proceeded to its transfer to police units for a thorough inspection and weighing of the drug.
In addition, the officials verified that the registration had been "doubled" since it did not coincide with the collection in the documentation of the van, so there has also been charged to the arrested crime of falsification of public documents.
Ertzaintza continues with the investigation to proceed with the arrest of the other suspect, who managed to escape to the race as has been said, and to determine the provenance of the drug apprehended.
The detainee, 28-year-old A. A.J., was transferred to police facilities to carry out the corresponding proceedings and will go to court in the coming hours.
Actress Heather Locklear arrested for driving under the effects of medication
Actress Heather Locklear, the popular Amanda of the series Melrose Place, has been arrested this weekend in Santa Bárbara (California) for driving under the effects of some medication.
A witness saw her perform strange maneuvers while trying to get out of her parking lot in Montecito, according to a spokesman for the California highway patrol.
The witness has detailed that around 16.30 hours, Locklear "was stepping on the accelerator thoroughly, making a lot of noise, trying to take the car out of the car park back and forth, and as he went backwards he passed several times over his sunglasses."
Shortly afterwards, the witness, who apparently had not at first recognized the actress, saw that Locklear stopped on a nearby highway and got out of the vehicle.
This person was the one who notified the emergency services because "she was concerned about Locklear's life."
When the police patrol arrived, she found the actress sitting inside her vehicle, who was partially blocking the road.
"She seemed disoriented", so the agents took her to the specialized drug and alcohol center for a test.
According to a police spokesman, the actress was cooperative and the excess alcohol was ruled out from the first moment, although "according to the officers at first sight, we believe that Locklear was under the influence of some medication."
Locklear was arrested on suspicion of driving under the effects of some unspecified substance and entered the local prison around 19.00 but was released some hours later.
Two months ago, Locklear left an Arizona treatment clinic where he was recovering from an episode of anxiety and depression.
Llamazares urges the Andalusian IU federation to assume its competences in the Silva case
The general coordinator of IU and deputy of Izquierda Unida, Gaspar Llamazares, assured today that it will be the regional federation of the formation that will have to make a decision on the case of the former councilor of Youth and Sports of the City of Seville, Francisco Manuel Silva, who resigned last week after he was accused of carrying out deal of favor to relatives.
"For these things I am very federalist, and it will be the Andalusian organization that has to assume its competences," Llamazares said about the action that the party will have to adopt with which until now councilor in the Sevillian consistory.
However, the IU leader defended that the need for "transparency" to be present in the possible investigations into the irregularities that Silva may have committed: "IU has always defended that this type of issues have the highest degree of transparency."
In the same vein he stressed that the formation has "strict ethical codes for all public offices."
'May Blood', Garci's epic bet
Inspired by the National Episodes of Benito Pérez Galdós, José Luis Garci recreates the famous uprising of the Spaniards against the French in Blood of May, which is the most ambitious film in his filmography with about 15 million euros of budget.
In this way, the filmmaker has tried to "illustrate the torrent of characters and social analysis" that he has always admired in the work of the well-known novelist, he explained today during the presentation of the film, with Quim Gutierrez and Paula Echevarría at the head of a wide cast of performers.
May blood, with a footage above two and a half hours, premieres this Friday in the movie theaters, to become in the future in a miniseries for Telemadrid of more than four hours.
Great admirer of Galdós literature, José Luis Garci claims to have fulfilled this film "a childhood dream", which must thank Esperanza Aguirre, who from the Community of Madrid commissioned him three years ago to shoot a commemorative film for the second centenary of May 2, something he accepted as long as the story was based on the National Episodes.
The 15 million budget that the Community of Madrid has contributed in its entirety constitute for Garci a "unique opportunity" to set with the decorations and the appropriate costume the portrait of "a stunny Spain that was mistaken as boss to fight for, since, the struggle for independence led the people to enormous delay with the reign of Ferdinand VII", he said.
But the filmmaker regrets not having had funding such as Alatriste or The Ghosts of Goya - more than twice that of Blood of May-, so he has not been able to shoot some scenes captured in a script signed with Horacio Valcárcel, with whom he has collaborated in almost his entire career.
For this reason, the director of Back to start, has stressed what is his usual form of work, the staging with the actors, with whom he usually rehearses before starting the shooting and to those who does not casting, for appearing "humiliant" and consider that "part of the office of the director resides in going to the theater and the cinema to know what happens in the industry in which he works."
Many of the interpreters with whom he coincides in Sangre de May repeat before his camera, as is the case of Miguel Rellán, Carlos Larrañaga or Fernando Guillén Cuervo.
An Oscar candidate on four occasions - of which he won one-, Garci considers that any of the three candidates to represent Spain this year before the Hollywood Academy, the Blood of May itself, Seven French billiard tables and the finally selected, The Blind Sundivers, do not represent a young generation of Spanish cinema, "which is what must be made known," he said.
Garci doesn't expect awards with this movie.
"In the beginning you think that you work for posterity, then you realize that you only work for prosperity, and now you settle for making one more film," Garci explains after more than 30 years of professional career.
Isabel Bayón recovers the essence of Sevillian dancer Tórtola Valencia
A Sevillian dancer goes to pay tribute to a ballerina also Sevillian.
Carmen Tórtola Valencia (Florenç Tórtola Ferrer) and Andalusian mother (Georgina Valencia Valenzuela), was born in the Triana neighborhood and when she was three years old he moved to London with his family.
She was a free-dance dancer and specialist in oriental dances.
She had an exciting life, a pioneer in women’s liberation, such as Isadora Duncan, Virginia Woolf or Sarah Bernhardt.
She was a Buddhist and vegetarian and advocated the abolition of corset that impeded the free female movement.
The life of this woman that marked a dancistic and aesthetic revolution in the Europe between wars, fascinated Isabel Bayón.
"I wanted to work with Pepa Gamboa and she proposed this character to me and I found it amazing."
According to the dancer, "we don't intend to tell his life but to collect his spirit.
The titles of his dances have suggested flamenco sticks.
Thus, "the butterfly dance" suggested a guajira, or "the dance of the snake" some tangos.
His dance was sensual and I have been told that my dance is also within this aesthetic, "he says.
The montage has two exceptional collaborations: that of Miguel Poveda who will sing to Isabel again in one in his shows, as he did in that of "The open door"; and with the teacher Matilde Coral, who exceptionally has wanted to collaborate with his student.
"Matilde makes Tortola older, the character of maturity.
I’m happy because I’ve agreed to act in my work and I’m very honored for it.”
The montage also has the musical composition and guitars of Jesús Torres and Paco Arriaga, the cante of Miguel Ortega, el Pulga and Moi de Morón; the percussion of El Pájaro, as well as the viola of Rafa El Viola and the collaboration of an actor, Juanfra Juárez, "who plays the role of the eternal companion of this type of artists who were divas in their entirety, and who are always in love with them."
In the work the life of this woman that developed in such distant places, especially for the time, such as India, the United States, or Russia, in addition to all of Europe, is also told, since she habitually resided in London.
"I wanted to remember it because I think it's one of those women who have an enormous influence in a certain time and now no one knows and much less values their work," Bayón says.
The show will begin today at 20.30 at the Lope de Vega theater.
The publication of the "Diary of a Newlywed Poet" also closes the Trienium
The presentation of the first illustrated edition of the "Diary of a Poet Recencasado" in Saint Stephen's Church (New York), where Juan Ramón Jiménez married Zenobia in March 1916, is part of the activities scheduled by the Ministry of Culture of the Junta de Andalucía to celebrate the "Trienio Zenobia and Juan Ramón" (2006-2008), organized in order to commemorate the 50th anniversary
In this way, the "Trienio Zenobia and Juan Ramón" comes to an end with the reissue of this work, with the International Congress that has been held in New York, with the restoration of the Nobel Museum House and with the itinerant exhibition "Juan Ramón Jiménez: That poplar of light", which was inaugurated last May and which, after traveling through the different Andalusian provinces
Likewise, the Ministry of Culture of the Junta de Andalucía has acquired a total of eight hundred copies of the "Diary of a Recenned Poet", edited by Pedro Tabernero and illustrated by Jacobo Pérez-Enciso, to continue the dissemination of the figure and the work of one of the most universal Andalusian poets.
Ameria is less liked without Paul Newman
“Sometimes God creates perfect people.
Paul Newman was one of them."
The phrase is from actress Sally Field, who worked with him on "Absence of Malice", and gives the idea of the helplessness of American cinema, and throughout America, in the face of the shocking idea that Newman is gone.
Look, they had a hard time giving him an Oscar.
And he was retired since 2007.
And that it was known that he was very ill.
It's still been a sledgehammer for everyone.
"Sometimes what you feel is beyond all expression, and now I've lost a great friend," Robert Redford cried, "my life and my country were better thanks to their existence."
No one was afraid to make a ridicule or exaggerate.
A father had died of cinema and homeland in a particularly dark moment, in fear of terrorists and markets, with American self-esteem on the ground, desperately seeking reasons for dignity.
When it’s all the hard time and without virtue the men and well few beings to admire are left, you don’t have to be Luis Cernuda to love Paul Newman.
Embodied for everyone an almost Platonic ideal of beauty and of truth.
He was the formal enemy of Richard Nixon and an advocate for lost and earned causes, from the founding to combat drugs created in honor of his firstborn dead overdose to his brand of homemade and organic food.
A whole gift to a country that has struggled a lot to overcome a suicidal food culture.
The expansive wave of admiration swept Hollywood without attending to nationalities or passports.
The last James Bond, the Englishman Daniel Craig, found that he has not died a man but an era.
Another Englishman, Oscar-winning director Sam Mendes, said that working with Newman was nothing more or less than the peak of his career.
Australia’s bad boy Russell Crowe dared to call Newman his biggest inspiration.
"He has left the bar very high for us all," said George Clooney, perhaps one of the few who today dares to try to live as Paul Newman lived: keeping high the demand for probity and having a serious private life and away from the cameras.
This isn’t always easy or even possible in today’s media jungle.
But there are those who try, and Paul Newman is his example.
He divorced once but then he was married to Joanne Woodward, who was faithful when he surely had plenty of opportunities not to.
"But why am I going to eat burgers away from home, if I have steak inside?" he used to joke himself.
In heaven he is proud that on earth his daughters coincide with his fans, something not so common in America.
"Of all the great roles he played, none of whom he was as proud as that of devoted husband, loving father, solicitous grandfather and dedicated philanthropist," they say.
Impercent race of an Olympic Madrid
Yes, the dream of many of us is to be held in Madrid an Olympics and, after the failed 2012, it may be 2016.
But I wonder, are we ready?
If we follow the example of yesterday’s race, I say no!
The perfect race was called, and in my opinion it has been the worst race with a lot I've been in.
A height of nonsense that, of course, if taken into account as an evaluation and I examined, would be a very clear suspense.
But let's go in parts and from the beginning.
Nonsense begins with the collection of the dorsal, which makes you have to go sooner.
Which I don’t think it’s wrong, but if you’re already made to give it their all (including the chip to avoid queues in the race).
Well, not.
Here they only made you go to pick up the bib (first nonsense, go only for that) and (after the pertinent queue), surprise!, there was only size 'S' for the shirt (second nonsense, because I need a size XL)
What world do we live in?
Or have they decided to buy only small T-shirts with fewer fabrics and cheaper?
Well, things like these are minucia compared to race day.
Apart from having to go very soon to collect the chip, the queue on arrival is 15 minutes and, in addition, you have to leave the backpack.
More than 35 minutes lasted the wait, somewhat incomprehensible for a race where more than 11,000 people are expected.
Then, unsurprisingly, the race began and we had not yet left the backpack, although once achieved, the collection was worse.
In addition, the rain enlivened the wait of more than 40 minutes, which came together with accounts of popular rebellion and assault on the booth.
I think that about 500 people surely tomorrow we will have pneumonia, and some like me, they will not even appear with the right time in qualifying, due to the chip they gave me that, I imagine, did not mark my exit correctly.
Of course, if you asked me about the perfect race, it wouldn’t get into my thoughts.
So I think we'll keep dreaming of a well-prepared Olympic city.
Microsoft seeks receptionist (virtual)
At MIT Microsoft’s Emerging Technologies conference he has shown an advance of a new system of image recognition and human interaction, a kind of Artificial Intelligence without much intelligence but able to perform some basic tasks, such as asking for a taxi (or in the case of Microsoft, a shuttle to move around the company’s campus) according to what he hears in the conversation.
Thanks to a camera and a system of recognition of shapes and faces, this "receptionist" can know how many people there are in the room, who they are - even remember what they're wearing - and listen and understand the keywords of their conversations.
Also group those present according to conversation groups.
In the sample video the receptionist detects a third person but understands that he does not belong to the group that is talking to her and that he is not an employee of the company.
He asks him if he is there to visit someone and tells him that he will attend you shortly.
All without blue screening.
Impressive.
That, of course, is the future.
Now the company has better things to focus on, for example in the next version of its operating system, Windows.
This week has spoken well above a change of strategy at Microsoft.
Windows 7 will be lighter and won't have as many associated programs.
Steve Ballmer has found that if computers came with the must-have instead of loaded with commercial garbage, they’d go five times faster - man, Steve, it’ll be for less.
A complete finding taking into account that Microsoft, version after version, has added functions and "extras" to Windows.
The change of course is appreciated.
Microsfot is right, the operating system is losing importance, and so is the software.
When Google Chrome went online, I was one of those who added to the theory that it was Google’s first step in competing with Windows.
Many rightly pointed out that a browser is not an operating system.
The operating system will always be necessary because it is the essential layer for Hardware and Software to be understood.
It fulfills the same function as your wife or husband when you have to say something to your mother-in-law.
It's true.
It will never disappear but you have to think of the "death" of the operating system not as an absolute truth, but relative.
What Ballmer has said this week is the confirmation of this idea.
People are paying less and less attention to the operating system, and more to the web browser.
Your computer boots up just to run the Firefox or Explorer - or Chrome - and every day we spend more time navigating than in the window environment.
What are half of the applications that come in the system for?
Less is more.
And Windows 7 has to be less, much less, than Vista.
Less heavy, less recharged, less complex.
Microsoft is more interested in integrating its online services and that they are finally an alternative to Google.
They desperately need it, in fact, and I have a small example.
The photo of this post was taken in one of Microsoft’s employee break rooms in Redmond at the beginning of the month.
In the soda machine a poster asks them to use their search tool - "Your search. Our future".
In 2006, an independent study concluded that 66% of Microsoft employees used Google to search the network.
Last week, by the way, a delay in the new version of Windows Mobile 7 was also confirmed.
It will not arrive at the beginning of 2009 but at the end, rather throwing in 2010.
In Microsoft they will surely look for the joint launch of Windows 7 and Windows Mobile 7.
After all, the phone is more and more computer.
But the delay, with Android and the iPhone already on the street, is going to take its toll.
Le Fabuleux Marcel
Pablo Picasso wore it on the Côte d’Azur, Leonardo di Caprio wore one in Edward Zwick’s ‘Bloody Diamond’, the same as Al Pacino in Francis Ford Coppola’s ‘Blood Diamant’ and is part of the musculated gay parish uniform.
And if we listen to stellar apparitions, we can go back to the image of that pointed mustache that lifted weights in a traveling circus.
In essence, it’s nothing more than the typical men’s indoor t-shirt in cotton, snugged and sleeveless.
The 'tank', which they call the Anglo-speakers, but le Fabuleux Marcel de Bruxelles stands out from all the others.
First, because of its bombasticking name - although it is familiarly known as 'Marcel' - which has to do with the sport because it receives its name, according to the legend, of the boxer Marcel Cerdan, lover of the singer Edith Piaf.
'La Marcel' stands out over the others for its excellent cotton, for its unisex cut, for its distinctive shield and for the small Belgian flag (just recognizable) that the most emblematic T-shirt models have as a detail on their necks.
And attention, because the Belgian firm’s production doesn’t just focus on its tank tops.
It also includes college sweatshirts, retro boxers, cashmere cardigans, cotton trousers, sailor T-shirts, babies, mini shorts...
Garments designed for underwear comfort.
Although it's a shame to leave them like underwear.
They're too charming.
"I want to be a young mom; the candidate doesn't worry me"
His enormous resemblance to his sister has not prevented him from developing his own personality.
Mónica Cruz is small but full of vitality.
Currently the Cruz sisters take their new activity in fashion very seriously.
For Monica, they are much more than some beautiful and famous girls who lend their name.
He has been in Paris in the promotion that the firm Mango does in its new headquarters, in addition to the presentation of the most important fashion awards that the Spanish house sponsors.
Warm and shy, win on short distances.
Tell us what you're doing in Paris.
I have come to present the new collection that my sister Penelope and I have devised for Mango in this new autumn-winter season, in addition to attending the presentation of the second edition of the 'Mango Fashion Awards' and the Opera dinner in honor of Valentino.
Come on, a very intense day but, at the same time, exciting.
What is your new collection based for Mango in this fourth season working for the Spanish house?
Both my sister and I love the 50’s and 60’s cinema; that’s why we draw a lot of references.
Then, I really like to get lost in a city and observe women how they are dressed, how they combine and mix their clothes, I do a lot.
Dancer, actress and designer, you're a very multifaceted woman, aren't you?
I think yes but, in the end, everything comes together and they have a lot of ties in common.
From a young age I liked clothes; I wanted to go to my grandmother’s house to delve into my aunt’s closets and see her clothes.
It was the bomb, almost better than going to the amusement park.
Then, as a dancer, she always tried to take care of how I dressed.
You know that dancers really like to do our outfits for classes and rehearsals, to look at ourselves in the mirror and feel good.
And in the role of actress when they give you the wardrobe is when you really find the character.
So, everything goes together, of course, with a lot of respect for designers and professionalism.
You've fulfilled not long ago 30, have you had a crisis?
What's going.
I feel better than ever, I like 30.
I find that women are made, have experience and savor life with fewer mistakes.
The biological clock doesn't scare me.
I’ve done the things I’ve wanted since childhood and this has given me a lot of inner happiness.
So do you think about being a mother or not?
Of course, I know that I am going to be a mother because the body asks for it and, in addition, I want to be a young mother; this I do not want to miss it.
As for the candidate, I don't worry.
What I'm going to do is make my son very happiness and educate him with a lot of love.
As for the cinema, you’re aware of several premieres you shot last year.
That's right and I'm looking forward to seeing the light.
I, who am a woman who comes from the theater, I am very impatient and restless, I want everything now and of course, cannot be.
I’m looking forward to ‘The Last Hour’ and ‘In Search of the Tomb of Christ.’
Let's see what you think...
A few days ago you debuted on a runway with Naomi Campbell, didn't you?
Well, well, don't say that.
It's not that I went on a catwalk to model.
He simply called me Naomi Campbell, whom I’ve known for a long time, to invite me to go to London to participate in a beneficial parade for his organization ‘Women for a better world’ and the clothes we exhibited – I took out two pieces from our collection for Mango – was auctioned to raise money for this Naomi NGO.
People don't know Naomi's tender heart.
So many things are said about her that, I know her, I'd tell you that many aren't true.
Besides, I'll stick with that good part where she gets involved.
He asked me to parade for such a noble cause, but if he asks me to do the pine, then too.
To benefit third-world women, whatever it takes.
Recidive drivers for breathalyzer must install the 'alcolock'
Interior wants the driver with three administrative penalties for breathalyzer, has to install in his vehicle a system that prevents starting the drunk vehicle, or if it is not blown.
The initiative was already announced last June and is now included in the annual report of the State Attorney General’s Office, according to which it is a pilot project that will last a year.
Its purpose is the reintegration of the driver, so he will also receive a road re-education course.
In the experience, in addition to the Attorney General’s Office, the Directorate General of Traffic (DGT) and the Technological Institute for Automobile Safety (FITSA) will participate.
According to statistics, between 30% and 75% of motorists reported more than once for drunk driving catches the car.
Alcolock would avoid it since it is a system connected to the vehicle that measures the rate of alcohol in expired air and prevents starting the car if the driver does not bleak abs; or when a concentration of alcohol is detected is higher than allowed.
Its cost is more than 1,000 euros and it would take account of the sanctioned.
In this sense, a study carried out in the United States in 2007 showed that vehicles equipped with these devices reduce violations by up to 65% during the first year of implementation.
And in Spain it is estimated that it could save the lives of 115 people every year.
The measure adheres to administrative penalties for two reasons: to address those behaviors before they result in criminal offences.
And to facilitate its implementation, since the legal reform can be done by ordinary means, and yet, the necessary reform by criminal means requires a reform in an organic law.
The device is not yet available on the Spanish market in any commercial modality, although it has already been experimentally implemented in some commercial and public service vehicles.
Precisely tomorrow, the Congress of Deputies will vote tomorrow an initiative of the PSOE in which it intends to install on school buses this system, as has been carried out in France.
In Mexico and without 'peep-toes'
With British punctuality, at 17.40, the scheduled time in the program, landed in the presidential Hangar of the Benito Juárez airport of Mexico DF the plane of the Spanish air force in which the Princes of Asturias were traveling.
The sun was retreating but its beams still forced to use the hand as a visor to see the real couple descend from the plane.
At the foot of ladder, the Spanish ambassador and other authorities.
Hours earlier he had asked about the street, to the taxi driver, if he knew that the Spanish Princes visited the city to formalize an official trip.
- Ah, very good, then you won't be able to go to the airport to charge at that time... she's from here or so...
- No, not sir, she's Spanish, but she lived here for a couple of years.
- Ah, very good...
But the Mexican press does know very well who the Princes of Asturias are and the importance of the trip.
There are many waiting in the airport hangar attached to the red carpet and a military fence made up of 27 navy soldiers who play the salvo of honors.
The Prince appears smiling, dressed in navy blue, and she, the Princess, very soberious nail, does not use her usual 'peep-toes', perhaps she had been informed that in the DF he threatened a storm.
They give a smile and a brief stop to the photographers before moving on to the room in which the official entourage awaits.
At the door of the hotel in which they are staying, one of the big four in the city, a blissore reporter from Mexican television awaits them.
The best gifted prize in fashion
Possibly there is no place in the most appropriate world to present a fashion award, that Paris and there may not be a more exquisite living designer and bont live than Valentino.
Mango has just presented the second edition of El Botón-Mango Fashion Awards, to enhance the international projection of the new talents of fashion.
It is endowed with a prize of 300,000 euros.
Yesterday Sandrina Fasoli, the Belgian winner of the first edition, looked at her designs in Mango’s showcase at number 54 of the Boulevard Haussmann in Paris and almost wept with emotion.
By the way, it is the second largest store of the firm in Europe and has just been inaugurated with 1,335 square meters.
The first one is in London.
So many events, together with the desire to thank the designer Valentino who presided over the jury of the contest, caused us to finish at a luxury dinner at the Garnier opera in Paris.
With the proximity of the most exquisite Chagals, the presidential gala table prepared by Mango, looked even more spectacular.
Nati, with a broken heart of grief and making guts heart, for the recent death of her brother, put on a costume of the House and some dressings of ELAH, which cut their breath, and the arm of his son Luis - a canyon of almost two meters in length - accompanied Valentino in the presidency of his homage dinner.
Andik, owner and lord of Mango, did the honors and is so gentleman that it ended, next to the baron of Rotchil, without a jacket, because he had both lent them to two ladies.
Mónica Cruz, who went to the store with pants and shirt - of her own design - and for dinner she was transformed with a sexy long black dress, was thrilled when in the night walk through the opera, accompanied by Goya Toledo, they showed us the rehearsal room of ballet and she, remembering other times, grabbed one of the bars and made a friend of 'grand plie'.
Inés Sastre didn’t stay for the walk through the palace’s outbuildings, but earlier he showed us photos of his son’s mobile phone.
The next morning we were able to rejoice in the presence of Olivier Martinez - another cannon that travels by motorcycle - and by John Travolta, who arrived at the intercontinental hotel in Paris, with his sheets, his towels and having previously requested that there be no odors in his room.
Stars under the starry sky of Paris.
Oh là là, c'est la vie.
1001, the anti-stress number
There are not 1000 but 1000 more a way to forget the chaotic and frantic everyday life.
And if that’s not enough, they’re all gathered in a book that will bring relaxation to bookstores on September 30.
The alarm clock almost punishes when it sounds every morning at 7 a.m., but it’s even worse to imagine getting wrapped up in the hectic rhythm that current life imposes.
The office is called tension and the computer back pain.
Stress is the evil of the 21st century.
But there are none or two methods to combat daily exhaustion and the problems of disconnection with the world of work.
No, the number amounts to, nothing more and nothing less than 1001.
Apart from the positions of yoga, the classic massage or the treatments of 'spa', Susannah Marriott gathers in the pages of a book a 'menu' of possibilities adapted to the taste and minutes of each one.
The anti-stress work is called '1001 ways to relax' and its calm will reach the bookstores by Pearson Alhambra and DK.
If someone considers that it is not possible to reach tranquility in areas such as work or through personal relationships, this ‘manual’ denies such a belief.
In addition, it offers other procedures such as being in contact with nature or 100% natural recipes that contribute to relaxation.
If adrenaline no longer runs through your veins to restore body balance and stress has become a problem, it’s time to sit on the couch, disconnect the mind, and focus on reading.
Speaking asleep
Some kids talk at night.
I don’t mean those who wake up very cleverly telling us something that has happened the day before, but children who speak while asleep.
The first time it happens, maybe you’ll scare, but it doesn’t really matter.
It's a benign disturbance of sleep called somniloquies.
It is quite frequent, especially in children and adolescents.
Surely more than one have been told that he spoke at night.
This phenomenon can appear very sporadically (once in life for example associated with an episode of high fever), or more periodically (every day, once a week, once in monthS...), the next day the child remembers nothing.
There is no unique cause that determines its appearance.
They are often related to other sleep disturbances such as nightmares, nocturnal terrors, nocturnal enuresis (sleeping) or sleepwalking, but it does not have to be so.
As we have already mentioned above, they can also be associated with very high fever, stress or overexcitement situations.
It's an unstudied phenomenon anyway.
The intensity with which it appears is also very variable.
It may speak in a calm tone of voice, loose words, a murmur or the opposite (screaming, cries, long speeches...).
In cases where the manifestation is very intense it would be advisable to observe the child and consult with a specialist, to check if there is any question that is generating a lot of anxiety and gives him out in this way.
We have said that it is a benign disorder, therefore it has no treatment, unless it is associated with another sleep disturbance that can be treated (for example, if it is associated with sleep apnea or a stress picture).
What you can do is to take into account a series of very simple preventive measures such as:
Try not to excite the child much before going to sleep.
Therefore look for quiet games and avoid programs or TV series that are scary or a little violent.
With very nervous children who have a hard time relaxing, relaxation techniques can be used to help them acquire a certain internal calm.
This will encourage the child to have a more peaceful sleep.
Once the somniloquias appear, try to stay calm.
Recall that they are harmless to the child and that the next day you won’t remember what has happened.
Useful tips
Don't wake him up.
It's useless, because he's not conscious, and you'll break his sleep.
Even though he's talking, he's sleeping and so he's resting.
Remember that if you are very distressed for several episodes you should consult or find out what generates so much anxiety in the child.
If you understand what he says (which is not the most usual thing), do not fall into the temptation to do an interrogation to draw information from him.
You won't get anything, because being asleep he's not conscious enough to organize his thinking.
In addition you will hear what it says, but you do not know what it is dreaming to say that, with which a lot of care in how you interpret the information and with the conclusions you draw.
Never tell the child that you can interrogate him at night to know his secrets.
Not even joking.
It generates a lot of anxiety.
In short, don’t worry, it’s nothing wrong, it’s an episode that lasts just a few minutes.
