Sarkozy Finds Furious Fishermen
Before taking off towards the United States, Nicolas Sarkozy will devete around Brittany.
Faced with the manifestation of resistance on the part of the fishermen who strongly disturbed last Monday the entries of several Breton and Norman ports, the president of the Republic decided to go last Tuesday morning to the port of Guilvinec, in the Finistère, to find their representatives there.
They pledged to welcome him " in a constructive spirit " and hope that " it will come with concrete propositions " .
This presidential visit coincides with the general meeting of the " crisis committee " created by Breton fishermen to achieve measures in the face of the increase in oil prices, which sets a record by reaching 1.14 euros per liter.
According to Elysée spokesman David Martinon, the president “will discuss with them solutions that can be envisioned to help their companies to become competitive again and to save the conditions of jobs and remuneration of sailors.”
The fishermen await Nicolas Sarkozy on a firm foot.
On Tuesday morning, they carried operations to reduce circulation in the Morbihan and Calvados, which have caused traffic jams of almost 10 km.
Quiberon Marina has also been blocked, but connections to Belle-île haven’t stopped.
" A filtering barrier " has been placed on the Quiberon Peninsula.
The sailors already showed on Monday their determination by burning, by scratching dawn, tires and wooden pallets in front of the fuel tanks of the ports of Brest, and Lorient, while demonstrations took place in Concarneau or Saint-Brieuc.
The movement spread to other ports on the Atlantic coast and the Mancha, as in Les Sables d'Olonne, in Vandea, where the bous stayed in the port, or in Le Havre, where about twenty ships blocked the movements of trade vessels.
The fishermen of La Turballe and Croisic, in the Loire-Atlantic region, stopped in the afternoon the blockade of the major Total refinery of Donges.
Boulogne-sur-Mer fishermen decided to wait until this Wednesday’s meeting that will take place to the ministry to think about eventual actions.
In Guilvinec, the " crisis committee " will have to think on Tuesday about the development of the movement and its participation in the planned meeting at the Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries in Paris.
But some Breton fishermen don’t want to be represented by heads of the National Fisheries Committee, which didn’t sustain their movement.
" The strike will continue " because " the fishermen did not abandon " and " do not want to move towards Paris," said Liliane Cariou, the chairman of Lorient's fisheries committee.
Professional fishermen, who do not pay fees on diesel, currently pay about 50 cents per liter but consider that you can reach the financial equilibrium point of a boat only with a diesel oil with a price less than 0.30 euro per liter.
