Analysis of the historical session of the Paris Stock Exchange
The New York Stock Exchange charged a historic fall last Monday after the House of Representatives rejected Paulson’s blueprint to save the banks: the Dow Jones lost 6.98 percent, that is, a loss never seen at nearly 800 points, and the Nasdaq 9.14 percent.
The Dow Jones industrial index fell from 777.68 to 10,365.45 points.
In terms of points, the most important fall in its history (684.81 points) had been recorded on September 17, 2001, that is, the day on which the exchanges resumed after the terrorist attacks of September 11.
The Nasdaq index, to the dominant technology, fell 199.61 points to 1,983.73 units and the index widened (extended) Standard & Poor's 500 by 8.81% (i.e., 106.85 points), to 1,106.55 units, according to the definitive closing figures.
Already depressed by the crisis at the beginning of the session, Wall Street sank after the unexpected rejection by the House of Representatives of the plane to save the banks that would have allowed to recover some stability in the markets.
With 228 votes against and 205 in favor, the representatives denied U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson the means he asked for.
About two Republicans out of three rejected his plan.
"That's not a good signal sent to the market, that Congress sees unable to adopt a plan," said Lindsey Piegza of the FTN Financial.
"This shows the extent to which the market had this plan," Lindsey Piegza added, fearing that a multi-day project will arrive "too late."
“If the project is really dying ... the Fed and other central banks will quickly take over (the U.S. central bank, the N. of the Network) to manage the crisis, which would further tighten credit conditions,” said Brian Bethune, Head of Economics and Financies at Global Insight.
And further accentuating the feeling of urgency, " each passing day the fall of a financial institution " in the United States is announced, Mrs Piegza stressed.
After the bankruptcy of Washington Mutual last Friday, the authorities organized the purchase of Wachovia’s banking activities by rival Citigroup.
In Europe, the crisis accelerated dramatically, prompting emergency interventions for the benefit of banks in the UK, Benelux, Iceland and Germany, which shows that markets face “not only a crisis in the U.S. but of a global crisis,” added the FTN Financial analyst.
The obligatory market has risen dramatically.
10-year treasury bond yield fell 3,632%, up 3.827% on Friday afternoon, and the 30-year bond by 4,161%, up 4.357% the day before.
