“Next week could be a decisive episode,” Senator Charles Schumer said yesterday.
Rep. Rahm Emanuel, a former member of the Clinton administration, compared Obama’s “good economic instincts” with those of Bill Clinton during the 1998 financial crisis.
According to the Democratic candidate, the current financial disaster “is the final consequence of eight years of poor economic polity put in place by George Bush and sustained by Senator McCain.”
Reaffirming that it would nevertheless reduce taxes by 95 percent of Americans despite Wall Street shocks, the Illinois senator also pointed out that the realization of the country’s eventual energy independence thanks to the promotion of renewable energies was their first priority, with the creation of a safety and health system.
McCain wants to build a park of 45 nuclear power plants by 2030.
Prudentes, both candidates dodged the issues that referred to the 700 billion financial salvage plan (470 billion euros) adopted yesterday afternoon by Congress and extremely unpopular.
" In Minsk, " Have you noticed the elections in Minsk?"
Young people and opponents protested last Sunday afternoon in the main square of the Belarusian capital to reject in advance the results of the legislatures.
Some hundreds of Belarusians gathered on Sunday afternoon from the closure of polling stations in Minsk’s central square to protest against the fraudulent nature of legislation.
While no outcome had yet been announced, numerous young people had come to demonstrate against President Alexandre Loukachenko's power: "the dictator has to resign."
Some acknowledge not having voted: "for what, since they will do what they want with my vote," says a young man.
They do not believe that the regime that addressed foreign observers well in order to renew contact with the European Union is ready to effect democratic change.
Sanctions, among which visa interdiction for 40 Belarusian leaders and the freezing of certain assets, could be lifted if these elections are considered fair to observers.
They didn't do it according to the opposition.
“We have been placed alongside the electoral commissions and our observers could not participate in recounting the votes,” said Iouri Potemkine, the spokesman for the Union of Democratic Forces, a heterogeneous regrouping of twelve parties.
"It's a cosmetic change, says Alexandre Milinkievitch, a former candidate of the opposition to the 2006 presidential election and leader of the pro-European movement, because the important thing is who counts the votes, and in our house, it's the executive branch.
If some opposition deputies enter the parliament, it is because they will have been appointed but not elected.
" But his entry into parliament may mean television appearances, meetings in the regions and bills."
The opposition must therefore enter into the political game because " we must start the dialogue with the European Union.
Because if our economy, which has a lot of debt in front of Russia, goes into crisis, and if we do not reform ourselves, we are in danger of becoming a protectorate of Russia.
But we want to be independent."
The opposition fears that the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe that followed the electoral process will not recognise the validity of the vote.
Stresses that the turnout figures appear too high, and that the generalization of voting in advance (26% of voters in Minsk) favoured manipulations.
" Have you noticed the elections? Interrogate the Social Democrat leader Alexandre Kozouline, one of the last two political prisoners released in August of this year.
There was no one in the polling stations.
And there will be at most five deputies of the opposition to the parliament, those who are best suited to power. "
Silence of the night returns again in October Square.
On television, President Loukachenko and members of the central electoral commission welcome their success.
The interview - Blood is safe again
Héma-Québec was celebrating its 10th anniversary of its foundation yesterday, and its president, Francine Décary, was right to rejoice, because Héma-Québec has once again become one of the safest and most effective blood providers in the world.
And above all, he regained the confidence of the public that had seriously been faltered by the blood scandal contaminated in the 1980’s and 1990’s.
As is well known, national blood reserves administered by the Canadian Red Cross Blood Transfusion Service have been contaminated by Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) and Hepatitis C Virus during the 1980’s.
Faced with this drama and discontentation of the public, the Government of Canada held a public poll in February 1994, chaired by Judge Horacio Krever, which aimed to clarify the circumstances that had led to such a tragedy and make recommendations so that such a disaster does not reproduce further.
While Judge Krever was slow to present their relationship, certain provinces then created a committee that aimed to plan a new blood management system in Canada.
In Quebec, the Gélineau committee accomplished this mission.
As a result of Gélineau and Krever’s reports, two suppliers have been created in Canada: Héma-Québec to provide Quebec and the Canadian Blood Society to supply all other provinces and both territories.
In 1998, when Héma-Québec began its activities, almost 38% of people still had confidence in the blood system in Quebec, Décary recalls.
The situation was discouraging.
Having made many efforts to improve the safety of blood products and a lot of work during the years that followed, we have regained the confidence of the public," he says before proudly stating that at the beginning of 2008, about 94% of the Quebec population henceforth had confidence in Héma-Québec.
Dr. Décary recalls, however, that “confidence is something extremely fragile”.
Since its inception on 28 September 1998, Héma-Québec applied the precautionary principle.
At that time, we were witnessing in Europe the birth of numerous cases of the human variant of mad cow disease, a new variant of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, Décary says.
And we didn’t know if this disease could be transmitted by blood.
Then we rely on one of the main recommendations of Krever and Gélineau’s reports, according to which when we don’t know and when a serious disease can emanate from that, in this case Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease that is characterized by a degeneration of the brain that inevitably leads to death, we establish measures of exclusion.
In April 1999, we denied the blood of people who had spent more than a month in the UK since 1980.
But a few years ago it has been proven out of doubt that this disease is transmitted by blood.
This episode thus showed us that the precautionary principle that was applied was of meaning.”
In 1999, a much more sensitive HIV screening technique appeared on the market: nucleated acid tests that allowable to directly detect the presence of the virus and not the presence of antibodies against the virus, on which the tests used so far were based.
Nucleated acid tests shortened the period between the time the person has been infected and the time when the first viruses can be detected in the blood by our techniques, explains the specialist.
We were able to reduce this period by less than a week.
We thus substantially reduce the risk that certain donors will unknowingly be infected and that we cannot detect them.
These improvements made it possible to significantly reduce the risk of contracting AIDS due to a blood transfusion.
Today, one person every 12 million is at risk for HIV-contaminated blood.
"That means 12 million blood products will have to be distributed before someone is infected with HIV.
So since Héma-Québec distributes between 400 000 and 500 000 blood products each year, it will be more than 24 years before a case of transmission of HIV by a blood product occurs.
I think you can currently receive or prescribe a blood transfusion to regain health without worries, "says the head of Héma-Québec.
“Our challenge for the next decade is to maintain the safety of our products.
It’s never known which pathogen could emerge in the system.
Last year the Chikungunya virus appeared on Reunion Island, which, for example of the Western Nile virus, is transmitted by an insect in the blood.
You have to be constantly vigilant, "dr. Décary pursues before stressing that in addition to a safe supply, Héma-Québec must also foresee sufficient supply.
" Necessities increase because the population ages.
Since more aggressive anti-carcinogenic treatments, patients often need an injection of red blood cells or platelets.
Population ageing also has consequences on the network of blood donors regrouping on average only 3 per cent of Quebequeses admissible to blood donation. "
All people with good health, 18 years old and who correspond to admissibility criteria can however donate blood every 56 days, that is six times a year.
On average, 14% of people who propose their blood are not accepted, because they suffer from AIDS or malaria, for example, and if they traveled in a country where malaria reigns, they will be excluded for a period of one year.
" With this 3%, we managed to maintain an inventory that covers the needs of hospitals.
The hardships are very rare.
But we must renew the members of this network of donors as they age and can no longer donate their blood.
But it's hard to convince people to donate their blood, but most of all to retain them and incite them to be a regular donor.
Since today’s life is very rampant, we rarely find the time to spend an hour to donate her blood,” Francisne Décary grieves.
With a budget of 297 million dollars annually, Héma-Québec supplies not only blood products, but also human tissues, among which bone grafts, heart leaflets, eyeballs and skin that is almost exclusively used for large burns.
These tissues are analyzed, transformed and frozen before being stored in Hema-Québec, which also manages the only public umbilical cord blood bank in Quebec.
Umbilical cord that was formerly considered biomedical waste is full of a blood rich of stem cells that can be used for the treatment of serious diseases such as leukemia.
" We celebrated a few weeks ago, placing on the bench of our thousandth blood sample from the umbilical cord and it is expected to remove more than a thousand of these per year.
We would have to start proposing it for grafts at the beginning of next year, "says Doctor Décary who describes this cord blood-taking program as very popular.
“Many mothers are disappointed not to be able to enjoy this,” since Héma-Québec has no resources to propose this complex and expensive program in all Quebec hospitals.
