Charest takes care of the North
The prime minister does not preclude holding elections before the end of the year
As soon as the general council of the Liberal Party ends, Jean Charest was yesterday in Juan-Talon, in Quebec, where the new Minister of Health, Yves Bolduc (on the left), tries to make himself elected during today’s by-election.
Rejecting the limits of our last great northern border ": such is the long-term ambition that Jean Charest presented with visual force staying in the big lines, at the end of the general council of the Liberal Party of Quebec, in Lévis, yesterday.
Behind him, during his speech, images were projected.
They paraded, in three immense screens, spectacular images of hydroelectric reservoirs, of mining, Quebec seen by "Google Maps", maps, etc.
Great means as during the election period.
The prime minister thinks that the elections will take place this fall, although he showed that his schedule was very full.
The “Northern Plane” is the fourth side of a “vision” that the prime minister intends to have for Quebec, that of a “new economic space”, which comprises an agreement on labor with France, an alliance between Canada and Europe and harmonies with Ontario.
Often mentioned in the prime minister’s speeches recently, the opening of the North – the northern territory at 49° parallel – had never been the subject of a presentation.
The Prime Minister limited himself despite explaining why the North was "a jewel to the immense potential", but he did not give a term, investment amounts, or precise priority in future projects.
What could above all be more developed are: particularly hydroelectricity, the forest, but above all the mining sector, doped by the growth of emerging countries and the reconstruction of public infrastructures.
The North contains " nickel, zinc, copper, platinum, gold, silver, iron, titanium, and also diamond", according to the enumeration of Mr. Charest.
This “twice-big territory like France,” “is our home, it’s in Quebec.
It’s not only home, but it’s in us,” he insisted, to show that he was requesting a national founding myth.
Quebec was built a lot thanks to its relationship with the North, he underlined: the ride towards gold in Abitibi, Quebec engineering, James Bay.
"It belongs to us, it's our future," he added, having paused theatrical to contemplate the immense map behind him.
The North, 70% of the Quebec territory, comprises a part of the Labrador that Quebec always claimed, the prime minister said during the press conference.
" It's a traditional position that all governments reiterated.
There’s a border line they haven’t agreed on for a long time and, when the occasion arises, I reiterate that position on the part of the government.”
In addition, "with climate changes, we must rethink our vision of the North," he stated speaking of the northwest passage, which will open each year due to the smelting of the ices.
In this new geopolitical context, the prime minister believes that occupying the territory is a duty.
And the inhabitants of the territory, the First Nations, and the Inuites?
The prime minister said the Quebec government would be very attentive to them and that the project would benefit them, particularly young people.
Two former ministers who come from two different parties, Pierre Corbeil and Michel Létourneau (former deputy of the Quebec party of Ungava), already began consultations about thirty communities.
An inter-ministerial committee chaired by Benoît Pelletier and in which Julie Boulet (Transport and Natural Resources) has an important role began some work.
The prime minister does not exclude an important meeting, "not necessarily a summit", but a meeting in which "a common vision will be given".
Northern development would be done in accordance with the principles of sustainable development.
The Northern Plan “will be concerted, orderly and environmentally friendly,” he promised.
Jean Charest described this "vision" as one that is in continuity with that of the liberal prime ministers who preceded him.
“As the liberals who preceded us, we have thought otherwise to transform the challenges into new opportunities for growth and development.”
This was one of the recurring weekend themes, which began on Friday with a great cocktail to underline the 50th anniversary of Jean Lesage’s rise to party power.
One of the resolutions adopted proposes to rename the Central Beauharnois in Adelard-Godbout Reservoirs.
“De Godbout, who created HydroQuebec, in Lesage who made the Revolution calm and the nationalization of hydroelectricity in Bourassa made by James Bay our party knew how to forge the future,” Juan Charest said.
At the press conference, he invoked another reference, that conservative, the "Roads to the North" program of John Diefenbaker, prime minister from 1957 to 1963.
Touching on the Eastmain1a-Ruper hydroelectric reservoir project, Mr. Charest underlined that its commissioning is planned for 2012.
"This stays between us, but I expect to be there," he confided to his militants, drawing applause.
Indeed, the prime minister, who said in April that elections would not take place in 2008 and certainly not in 2009, did not rule out the possibility of launching a vote this fall, which the law allows.
He emphasizes that his schedule is nevertheless full, particularly because of a mission of the council of the federation in China and a Quebec-New York York summit.
The general weekend council supplied the liberal chief with a platform containing nationalist resolutions, particularly " constitutional aspirations " that had no problem being accepted: the Quebec Liberal Party claims in particular the recognition of the " specificity of Quebec " in the Constitution.
He also hoped that Quebec would appoint members of the Canadian Broadcasting and Telecommunications Council and that they would have a veto right on decisions taken in Quebec.
On the side of the opposition, we embraced the great lines of the Northern Plan with skepticism.
The head of the Québeques party, Pauline Marois, highlighted the Northern Quebec Development Policy that the Landry government had launched in 2001.
According to her, the liberal government, coming to power in 2003, resigned to apply it.
If it had, "we would make progress today," Ms. Marois said next to her candidate in Jean-Talon (Vote that took place today), Françoise Mercure.
Quebec’s Democratic Action seemed to support the adoption of a Northern Plan by the government.
"But what is desired is that this is not just a spectacle, "said leader Sebastien Proulx, replacing Mario Dumont, who returns on vacation today.
Lyrics - A Dishonored Country
It's Mr. Vic Toews, former "Justice" minister (oh irony!) in the Harper cabinet, who was allowed last summer to say that Luisa Arbour was an "embarrassment" for Canada.
Dishonore this woman who carried an incessant fight for justice and who deserves all our admiration what everything about the relations of the Prime Minister and Stephen Harper says.
We are in front of a government that is the only one, among all Western countries involved, that has not repatriated its national who is locked up in the Guantanamo concentration camp.
It is a government that does not take into account in any way the fact that Omar Khadr was a very young teenager when the events that are recriminated occurred.
The only government that, despite the fact that Canada signed the Geneva Convention, reneges its promise and leaves a child-soldier living in inhuman conditions.
