Students victims of the expensive rental, up to 900 euros for a room
The rent market for university students does not know the crisis.
According to the last Sunian survey, the tenants’ union, renting a single room could cost a student 900 euros in Milan, 700 in Florence, 650 to Napoles, 600 to Rome.
"The phenomenon has already assumed very serious characteristics to the point of excluding the right to study a large part of young people for the very high costs that the family would have to sustain - explains the head of the Sunian studies office, Laura Mariani - in these cities the strong demand has totally deformed the market to such an extent to trigger a process of widespread increase even for residents."
Apart from the fact that in most cases rental offers contain a series of violations: free-type, unregistered contracts, with no fee limit, related sub-rents.
Extraordinary interventions are necessary for Sunia, which, through the revision of taxation, can regulate prices.
Looking in detail at the survey, we see that in Milan, for a bed in Brianza area, they serve approximately 450 euros; for a single room from a minimum of 650 (Bande Nere zone) to an average value of 800 (Lambiate zone, Udine and Fiera) to a maximum of 900 (in Victoria zone).
In Florence a bed costs on average 350/400 euros, a room approximately 700.
In Bologna, where students are concentrated in areas near the University, they serve 250/280 euros for one bed in a double room and from 370 to 500 for a single one.
In Rome in the central areas near the University (San Lorenzo, Plaza Bologna) they ask you about 600 euros for a single room and 450 for a bed in a double room.
Only slightly lower values (550 euros for a single room) in areas close to other Universities (Ostiense and Cinecittà).
Economics are made only if peripheral area is exchanged: 300 euros for one bed and 450 for a single room in Prenestina, Centocelle area and surroundings.
In Naples for a bed they serve you 300/450 euros, for a room is spent from 400 to 600 euros, with the highest prices in the Polyclinical, Vomero and Colli Aminei areas.
In Bari for one bed they serve 250/350 euros, at least 350 for an individual.
in smaller cities the prices are lower in absolute endings, but it has a greater weight in the economy of the city.
In cities such as Perugia, where the phenomenon of rents to students and non-community is more frequent, a further anomaly is recorded: an increase of 25/30% of the canon to foreign students compared to Italians.
Farewell to the old Paradiso cinema, the cities cancel historic theaters
Historical movie theaters close favoring the Multiplex.
"A city without movie theaters in a blind city, it's like an eyeless face."
The image offered by director Giuseppe Tornatore with effectively what is happening in large Italian urban centers and also in provincial cities.
The process of marginalization of the cinemas, which with the birth of the great methicons in the periphery and the consequent disappearance of the monkey theaters in the central areas of the city, is an inevitable and indisputable event with all the characteristics of a true cultural crime.
To disappear are almost always historical places, full of memory, "it is as if a friend died, an acquaintance with whom a piece of place and so much emotion was shared", observes the director of the new cinema Paradiso, the film that twenty years ago won the cousin Oscar and the great prize of the jury to Cannes, which told the poetry of a provincial cinema and the nostalgia for the way of living the cinema that lost perhaps forever.
The figures given by Anec, the main association of merchants, speak clearly: in the last five years they have closed 316 traditional cinemas, and to pay the most are above all the theaters of the main cities: 10 less in Florence, 9 less in Milan, Naples, Bologna and Rome, 6 less in Turin and 5 in Palermo.
Among the regions most suffered by the closures are as first, Emilia-Romaña with 82 fewer cinemas, following Tuscany (44) and Lombardy (41).
It is a demonstration that is responsible for its disappearance is the addition of the multiplex, these three Italian regions that have registered the largest number of new methon cinemas: 195 more screens in Lombardy in the last five years, 81 in Tuscany than in Emilia-Romaña.
“The Italian exercise has developed irrationally,” observes Paolo Protti, president of Anec,” it would be useful if new structures were built but it would be necessary to ensure the permanence of those already existing, because when a city room closes, not all its frequenters are willing to be transferred to methicons.
Not to mention the lack of funds in favor of merchants: the state’s previous debt is approximately 10 million euros.
It is shameful from an ethical and moral point of view, that the government does not respect the laws that exist.
To sustain the sector it would serve between 16 and 18 million euros a year.”
Interesting the case of Bologna, the first Italian city that worried about the closures, decided to be repaired with the signing of a protocol between the municipality and Anec, the first of its kind in Italy to promote and protect the single-room cinema is in the historical center that on the periphery.
Among the established rules there are means of tax facilitation in favor of managers and advantages for the public at mobility terminations: spectators will be able to enter the limited traffic area and will have special rates for parking.
“It’s an initiative that I like,” observes Giuseppe Tornatore” and I hope that other cities will follow Bologna’s lead by putting other measures in place.
It is true, that to contrast the extra power of methicons, the traditional halls have to improve the quality of the sound and the image because the public today is more demanding.
The defense of traditional cinema is a subject that for me was always important: the closure of Bagheria cinema, the place of my childhood and adolescence, was the spark that convinced me to make the "New Paradiso cinema", which I already thought for 10 years.
I have lived in Rome for 25 years, and when I pass in front of the Rialto or the Quirinale de via Nazionale already closed, where I have lived so many shared emotions, so as not to see the posters blackened by dust, I turn to the other side.
Link between politics and 'ndrangheta, the mayor of Gioia Tauro has been arrested
"The mayor of Gioia Tauro was available to uniform the elections of the Municipality for those interested in the Piromalli coscade."
They are heavy to accuse that the magistrates of the Anti-Mafia Directorate of Calabria give to the mayor and deputy mayor of Goia Tauro Giorgio Dal Torrione and Rosario Schiavone, and the mayor of Rosarno Carlo Martelli, arrested this morning by mafia association.
Also arrested Gioacchino Piromalli, the elderly mediator between the clan and the public administration, and his grandson lawyer, homonymus of the boss, already convicted of mafia criminal association and to indemnify 10 million euros.
"Despising any legal and moral norms, and of good sense," the prosecutors write, the two local administrations had expressed a willingness to pay consultations to lawyer Piromalli.
"It would have been allowed to the mafia family - the judges write - to officially enter the interior of the municipality facilitating the great possibility of control and command of the public administration."
The president of the Calabrés Agazio Loiero regional board does not hide that "agreements between bad organized life there is a lot" in his region: "There are areas where the mafia intensity is stronger than in others, where the agreement is almost inevitable.
But there are so many local politicians who resist the pressure of the ‘ndrangheta, heroes who stay clean.’
Those arrested this morning are added to those of last July after the police had announced the cutting of heads of the Piromalli mafia family.
Since the end of last April, the municipal council of Gioia Tauro had been dissolved, already in 1991 and always due to alleged conditions between organized crime and the local administration, local politicians had been relieved of the commission to administer the municipality.
Giorgio Dal Torrione, mayor of the Udc at the time of the dissolution, after the ballotting to May 2006, had been elected as head of a center-right coalition.
A scandal exploded when the Prefecture ordered the dissolution of the municipal council.
I call the vice minister of the interior who at that time was Marco Minniti, accusing him of having organized everything for a political question, but the judges in the arrest warrant signed this morning explain that Piromalli had even come to ask for the variation of the A3 road project.
From the environmental interceptions made to Dal Torrione, the judges write, "the willingness to favor the demands of an entrepreneur, representative of a group very close to the Piromalli clan, uniforming the elections of the public administration on the interests of mafia families."
When the energy is born from the waves, the great ones come down to the stage
It is an exterminated site that occupies more than 70% of the planet’s surface and is now being discovered by the world’s large energy companies.
Waves, currents, tides are the new renewable sources on which the big ones like Chevron look.
The possibility of collecting energy from the movements of the sea has been studied for a hundred years and in the 70’s along with the first oil crisis, the investigations intensified.
When the price of low oil, in 1980, the idea of harnessing marine energy was secluded, but now that environmental cases and the new rise in oil boost the sector.
The World Energy Council (WEC) in London has estimated by 2007 that 15 percent of the world’s electricity need could be covered by implants that exploit the movement of waves from the sea.
This amount would be twice that currently produced by nuclear power plants.
And there would also be currents and tides.
Giants to attack.
The first company to bet on marine energy was Enersis, which in 2005 has sealed an agreement to build an implant in northern Portugal.
When the intention was announced it seemed a revolutionary step.
Two years away to bet on marine energy it is always more and among these companies that have always voted for traditional energy.
“Chevron is studying technology on ocean energy – the spokesman for a company associated with the U.S. oil colossus told Wall Street Journal – and is evaluating the possibility of cooperating with us.”
The cooperation in question is about obtaining a permit from the U.S. energy commission to carry out a project to exploit marine energy in Alaska.
It’s quite an important sign, if you consider that in Alaska the U.S. continues to extract oil and recently President Bush has activated a plan to carry out new drilling.
In addition to Chevron is Verdant Power Inc. of New York and Ocean Power of New Jersey to follow the path of energy that comes from the sea, the total invested so far, although they are not detailed indicate an increasing interest.
Technology to Develop.
Experts talk about a technology still at the beginning, because marine energy there is no doubt that it has a great environmental impact.
For now the impacts such as those Portuguese or the Scots are small and do not seem to create big problems to the ecosystem.
To produce energy on a large scale, a technology with a greater environmental impact must be used.
And the options are several: Ocean Power, for example, uses a boas system attached to each other.
The force of the waves, which moves the boas upwards and down becomes hydraulic pressure with pistons and cylinders inside them.
The pressure rotates a turbine which in turn feeds a generator.
Electricity is sent to the coast through submarine cables.
For now there are four implants of this type under development, waiting only for federal approval, apart from the energy, it could serve for a military installation.
Verdant Power, on the other hand, already produces energy for a shopping mall and for parking, using underwater turbines in New York’s East River.
The movement of the water when there are tides, rotates the blades of the turbines, creating a rotating movement that feeds the generator.
Verdant has said it has a long list of customers waiting for the authorizations needed to use energy.
After the waves, the tides.
A particular interest, they say in the Institute for the development of electrical energy, merits at this moment the potential of tides.
Although it is an intermittent phenomenon, tides are more preventable than the wind, the sun or the waves and therefore more reliable as a source of energy.
It’s mostly about tidal energy that states like New York, Maine and Alaska, together with others approaching shorelines, have invested US$7 and a half million in 2008 and an investment of 35 million is planned for 2009.
